window.LUMEN_LOAD({"id": "multivariable", "lessons": {"multivar-functions": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"why-more-than-one-input\" data-h=\"3\">Why more than one input?</h3>\n<p>Almost nothing interesting depends on just one number. The temperature in a room depends on where you stand: your <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> position and your height. The price of a house depends on its size, location, age and number of bedrooms. A neural network's loss depends on every one of its millions of weights. Single-variable calculus studied functions <span class=\"m\">f(x)</span> with one input. Multivariable calculus studies functions like <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> or <span class=\"m\">f(x_1, \\ldots, x_n)</span> with many inputs, and almost every idea from one dimension (limits, derivatives, optimization, integrals) has a natural extension. This first lesson is about simply <em>seeing</em> such functions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario-a-hiking-map\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario: a hiking map</h3>\n<p>A hiking map is a perfect picture of a function of two variables. Each point on the map has an east–west coordinate <span class=\"m\">x</span> and a north–south coordinate <span class=\"m\">y</span>, and the ground has a height <span class=\"m\">h(x, y)</span> there. The map cannot show height directly, so it draws <strong>contour lines</strong>: curves joining points at the same height, say every 50 metres. Where contours bunch together the ground is steep; where they spread out it is nearly flat; closed rings around a point mark a summit or a hollow. You already know how to read multivariable functions; we will just make it precise.</p>\n<h3 id=\"functions-of-two-variables\" data-h=\"3\">Functions of two variables</h3>\n<p>A function of two variables assigns one output to each input pair: <span class=\"m\">f : \\mathbb R^2 \\to \\mathbb R</span>, <span class=\"m\">(x, y) \\mapsto f(x, y)</span>. For example <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2</span> gives <span class=\"m\">f(1, 2) = 1 + 4 = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(0, 0) = 0</span>. The <strong>domain</strong> is the set of allowed inputs. For <span class=\"m\">g(x, y) = \\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}</span> we need <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 \\le 4</span>, so the domain is a disc of radius 2. For <span class=\"m\">\\ln(x + y)</span>, the domain is the half-plane <span class=\"m\">x + y &gt; 0</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Function of several variables</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A rule <span class=\"m\">f : \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R</span> that assigns a single real number <span class=\"m\">f(x_1, \\ldots, x_n)</span> to each point of its domain in <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb R^n</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"graphs-as-surfaces\" data-h=\"3\">Graphs as surfaces</h3>\n<p>The graph of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> is the set of points <span class=\"m\">(x, y, z)</span> with <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span>: a surface floating over the <span class=\"m\">xy</span>-plane. <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> is a bowl (a paraboloid) with its bottom at the origin. <span class=\"m\">4 - x^2 - y^2</span> is an upside-down bowl, a dome with its peak at height 4. <span class=\"m\">x^2 - y^2</span> is a <strong>saddle</strong>: it curves up along the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis and down along the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-axis, like a horse's saddle or a mountain pass. A plane <span class=\"m\">z = 2x + 3y + 1</span> is the multivariable version of a straight line.</p>\n<h3 id=\"slices\" data-h=\"3\">Slices</h3>\n<p>One powerful way to understand a surface is to slice it. Freeze <span class=\"m\">y</span> at some value, say <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>, and you get an ordinary function of <span class=\"m\">x</span>: for the saddle, <span class=\"m\">x^2 - 1</span>, an upward parabola. Freeze <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> instead and you get <span class=\"m\">-y^2</span>, a downward parabola. Those single-variable slices are where partial derivatives will come from in the next lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"contours-and-level-sets\" data-h=\"3\">Contours and level sets</h3>\n<p>Slicing horizontally instead, at a fixed height <span class=\"m\">z = c</span>, gives a <strong>level curve</strong> (contour): the set of points where <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = c</span>. For the bowl <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span>, the level curve at height <span class=\"m\">c = 4</span> is <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = 4</span>, a circle of radius 2. Heights 1, 4, 9 give circles of radius 1, 2, 3. Equal steps in height give circles that are <em>not</em> equally spaced (radii <span class=\"m\">1, 2, 3</span> for heights <span class=\"m\">1, 4, 9</span>), because the bowl gets steeper as you move outward. For the saddle, the level curves <span class=\"m\">x^2 - y^2 = c</span> are hyperbolas, and the level set at <span class=\"m\">c = 0</span> is the pair of crossing lines <span class=\"m\">y = \\pm x</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Level set</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">f : \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R</span> and a number <span class=\"m\">c</span>, the set <span class=\"m\">\\{\\mathbf x : f(\\mathbf x) = c\\}</span>. In two variables it is a level curve (contour); in three it is a level surface.</p></div></div>\n\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A contour map: each ring joins points of equal height. The rings of the bowl are circles that get closer together as the bowl steepens.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>For a function of three variables, <span class=\"m\">f(x, y, z)</span>, we cannot draw the graph (it would need four dimensions), but level sets are surfaces we can picture. For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + y^2 + z^2</span> they are spheres. Weather maps of temperature in the atmosphere work this way.</p>\n<h3 id=\"limits-and-continuity-in-two-dimensions\" data-h=\"3\">Limits and continuity in two dimensions</h3>\n<p>The idea of a limit carries over: <span class=\"m\">\\lim_{(x, y)\\to(a, b)}f(x, y) = L</span> means <span class=\"m\">f</span> gets close to <span class=\"m\">L</span> whenever <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> is close to <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>, approaching <strong>from any direction</strong>. That last phrase makes 2D limits subtler. Consider <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = \\frac{xy}{x^2 + y^2}</span> near the origin. Along the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis (<span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>) it equals 0. Along the line <span class=\"m\">y = x</span> it equals <span class=\"m\">\\frac{x^2}{2x^2} = \\frac12</span>. Two paths give two different values, so the limit does not exist. Polynomials and other functions built from continuous pieces are continuous wherever they are defined, so most limits can still be found by substitution.</p>\n<h3 id=\"functions-with-many-inputs\" data-h=\"3\">Functions with many inputs</h3>\n<p>In machine learning, inputs are vectors <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (x_1, \\ldots, x_n)</span> with <span class=\"m\">n</span> in the thousands or millions. We cannot draw those, but all the 2D intuition (surfaces, valleys, contours, saddles) carries over, which is exactly why we study the two-variable case so carefully. A linear model <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x + b</span> is a \"flat\" function whose level sets are parallel hyperplanes, and a classifier's decision boundary is the level set where its score equals a threshold.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Confusing a contour map with a picture of the surface is common: contours are a top-down view, not a side view. Another mistake is assuming contours for equally spaced heights are equally spaced; their spacing depends on steepness. For limits, checking just one or two paths can prove that a limit does <em>not</em> exist, but it can never prove that one does.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A function of several variables assigns one output to each input point. Its graph is a surface; vertical slices give single-variable functions and horizontal slices give level curves. Close contours mean steep ground. Limits in several variables must agree along every path. These pictures are the language for gradients, optimization and loss landscapes in the rest of the course.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-surface\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;saddle&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;slices&quot;, &quot;x0&quot;: 1, &quot;y0&quot;: 0.5}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A 3D surface you can rotate by dragging. The pink and orange curves are the slices through the chosen point.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the saddle <span class=\"m\">x^2 - y^2</span>, drag on the plot to rotate it. Find the direction in which it curves up and the one in which it curves down.\n2. Move the sliders <span class=\"m\">x_0</span> and <span class=\"m\">y_0</span> and watch the two slice curves move with the point.\n3. Switch to the bowl, the dome and <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span>, rotating each one.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the pink slice (with <span class=\"m\">y</span> frozen) of the saddle is an upward parabola and the orange slice (with <span class=\"m\">x</span> frozen) is a downward parabola, exactly as computed in the article. The bowl's slices both open upward; the dome's both open downward. <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span> is a saddle too, rotated by 45 degrees.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The colors are heights and the rings are contours. Switch surfaces to compare contour patterns.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the bowl, look at the spacing of the contour rings as you move outward.\n2. Switch to the saddle and find the crossing point of the contours.\n3. Try the banana-shaped Rosenbrock function and the ravine.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the bowl's rings are circles getting closer together, because the surface steepens; the saddle's contours are hyperbolas that cross at the saddle point. The ravine's stretched ellipses show a valley that is steep across and shallow along, a shape that makes gradient descent zig-zag in later lessons.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">f : \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,\\quad \\mathbf x = (x_1, \\ldots, x_n) \\mapsto f(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{graph: } \\{(x, y, z) : z = f(x, y)\\} \\qquad \\text{level set: } \\{(x, y) : f(x, y) = c\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x^2 + y^2 = c \\text{ (circles)} \\qquad x^2 - y^2 = c \\text{ (hyperbolas)} \\qquad ax + by = c \\text{ (parallel lines)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(a,b)}f(x, y) = L \\text{ must hold along every path to } (a, b)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two-path test</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> approaches different values along two different paths to <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\lim_{(x, y)\\to(a, b)}f(x, y)</span> does not exist.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If the limit existed and equalled <span class=\"m\">L</span>, then <span class=\"m\">f</span> would be close to <span class=\"m\">L</span> at all points sufficiently close to <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>, in particular at points on both paths. So the limit along each path would also be <span class=\"m\">L</span>, and the two path limits would be equal, contradicting the assumption.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find and describe the domain of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = \\sqrt{9 - x^2 - y^2}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The square root needs <span class=\"m\">9 - x^2 - y^2 \\ge 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 \\le 9</span>.</li>\n<li>The domain is the closed disc of radius 3 centred at the origin.</li>\n<li>The range is <span class=\"m\">[0, 3]</span>; the graph is the upper half of a sphere of radius 3.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Describe the level curves of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = 2x + y</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Level <span class=\"m\">c</span>: <span class=\"m\">2x + y = c</span>, i.e. <span class=\"m\">y = c - 2x</span>.</li>\n<li>These are parallel lines of slope <span class=\"m\">-2</span>.</li>\n<li>Levels <span class=\"m\">c = 0, 2, 4</span> are equally spaced lines, because a plane has constant steepness.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Show that <span class=\"m\">\\lim_{(x, y)\\to(0, 0)}\\frac{x^2 - y^2}{x^2 + y^2}</span> does not exist</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Along <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{x^2}{x^2} = 1</span>.</li>\n<li>Along <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{-y^2}{y^2} = -1</span>.</li>\n<li>Different path limits, so the limit does not exist.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + 4y^2</span>. (a) Evaluate <span class=\"m\">f(2, 1)</span>. (b) Describe the level curves <span class=\"m\">f = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">f = 16</span>. (c) Along which axis is the surface steeper, and how does the contour map show it?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">4 + 4 = 8</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">x^2 + 4y^2 = 4</span> is the ellipse <span class=\"m\">\\frac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1</span> with semi-axes 2 (along <span class=\"m\">x</span>) and 1 (along <span class=\"m\">y</span>); <span class=\"m\">f = 16</span> gives semi-axes 4 and 2. (c) Along the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-axis <span class=\"m\">f = 4y^2</span> rises four times faster than <span class=\"m\">x^2</span> does along the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-axis. On the map the ellipses are squashed in the <span class=\"m\">y</span> direction: contours are closer together along <span class=\"m\">y</span>, meaning steeper ground.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Loss landscapes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A model's loss <span class=\"m\">L(\\mathbf w)</span> is a function of all its weights. Visualizing a 2D slice of it (varying two directions in weight space) gives the \"loss landscape\" pictures used in research: bowls for convex models, and complicated terrain with valleys, plateaus and saddles for deep networks. Elongated contour ellipses signal ill-conditioning, which is why features are standardized before training.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Heatmaps and isolines</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Contour plots and heatmaps are standard in data visualization and scientific computing. The marching squares algorithm draws level curves from a grid of values by deciding, in each grid cell, where the contour crosses the edges using linear interpolation, the same idea used to extract surfaces from 3D medical scans (marching cubes).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Weather maps</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Isobars on a weather map are level curves of air pressure <span class=\"m\">p(x, y)</span>. Where isobars are packed tightly, pressure changes quickly over a short distance and winds are strong. Isotherms (equal temperature) and elevation contours on topographic maps are the same idea.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">f(3, 4)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">9 + 16</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The level curves of <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> are...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">circles centred at the origin</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">parallel lines</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">hyperbolas</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = c</span> is a circle of radius <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt c</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Which surface is a saddle?</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">z = x^2 - y^2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">z = x^2 + y^2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>It curves up in <span class=\"m\">x</span> and down in <span class=\"m\">y</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The level curve <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = 16</span> has radius...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{16}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">On a contour map, contours that are very close together indicate...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">steep terrain</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">flat terrain</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a summit</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Height changes a lot over a short distance.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Along <span class=\"m\">y = x</span>, the function <span class=\"m\">\\frac{xy}{x^2 + y^2}</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{x^2}{2x^2}</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "partials": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"one-direction-at-a-time\" data-h=\"3\">One direction at a time</h3>\n<p>For a function of one variable, the derivative measures how fast the output changes when you nudge the input. With several inputs, you can nudge each one separately. A <strong>partial derivative</strong> measures the rate of change of <span class=\"m\">f</span> when you move in just one coordinate direction, holding all the other inputs fixed. It is the simplest way to extend derivatives to many dimensions, and it is exactly what a neural network computes for every weight during training: how much would the loss change if I nudged this one weight a little?</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>The heat index <span class=\"m\">H(T, h)</span> (how hot it feels) depends on the air temperature <span class=\"m\">T</span> and the humidity <span class=\"m\">h</span>. A meteorologist might ask two separate questions. On a humid day, if the temperature rises by one degree with the humidity unchanged, how much hotter does it feel? And at a fixed temperature, how much does an extra percentage point of humidity add? These are the two partial derivatives, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial T}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial H}{\\partial h}</span>. Each isolates one cause.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition\" data-h=\"3\">The definition</h3>\n<p>The partial derivative of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> with respect to <span class=\"m\">x</span> at <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span> is\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}(a, b) = \\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a + h, b) - f(a, b)}{h}.</span>\nOnly <span class=\"m\">x</span> moves; <span class=\"m\">y</span> stays at <span class=\"m\">b</span>. Similarly <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}</span> nudges only <span class=\"m\">y</span>. Notation varies: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_x</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\partial_xf</span> and <span class=\"m\">D_1f</span> all mean the same thing. The curly <span class=\"m\">\\partial</span> (read \"partial\") reminds you there are other variables being held fixed.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Partial derivative</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_x(a, b) = \\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a + h, b) - f(a, b)}{h}</span>, the rate of change of <span class=\"m\">f</span> in the <span class=\"m\">x</span> direction with all other variables held constant. <span class=\"m\">f_y</span> and partials in more variables are defined the same way.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"how-to-compute-them\" data-h=\"3\">How to compute them</h3>\n<p>In practice you never use the limit: <strong>treat every other variable as a constant and differentiate as usual.</strong> For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2y + 3y^3</span>:</p>\n<p>To find <span class=\"m\">f_x</span>, pretend <span class=\"m\">y</span> is a number like 5. Then <span class=\"m\">x^2y</span> is (constant) × <span class=\"m\">x^2</span>, with derivative <span class=\"m\">2xy</span>, and <span class=\"m\">3y^3</span> is a pure constant, with derivative 0. So <span class=\"m\">f_x = 2xy</span>.</p>\n<p>To find <span class=\"m\">f_y</span>, pretend <span class=\"m\">x</span> is a constant. <span class=\"m\">x^2y</span> is (constant) × <span class=\"m\">y</span>, with derivative <span class=\"m\">x^2</span>, and <span class=\"m\">3y^3</span> gives <span class=\"m\">9y^2</span>. So <span class=\"m\">f_y = x^2 + 9y^2</span>.</p>\n<p>At the point <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_x = 4</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y = 1 + 36 = 37</span>. Moving in the <span class=\"m\">y</span> direction changes <span class=\"m\">f</span> about nine times faster than moving in the <span class=\"m\">x</span> direction there.</p>\n<p>All the single-variable rules still apply. For <span class=\"m\">g(x, y) = e^{xy}</span>, the chain rule gives <span class=\"m\">g_x = ye^{xy}</span> and <span class=\"m\">g_y = xe^{xy}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">h(x, y) = \\frac{x}{y}</span>, <span class=\"m\">h_x = \\frac1y</span> and <span class=\"m\">h_y = -\\frac{x}{y^2}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-geometric-meaning\" data-h=\"3\">The geometric meaning</h3>\n<p>Slice the surface <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> with the vertical plane <span class=\"m\">y = b</span>. The slice is a curve <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, b)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">f_x(a, b)</span> is the slope of that curve at <span class=\"m\">x = a</span>. Similarly <span class=\"m\">f_y</span> is the slope of the slice in the plane <span class=\"m\">x = a</span>. Standing on a hillside, <span class=\"m\">f_x</span> is how steep the ground is if you face east, and <span class=\"m\">f_y</span> is how steep it is if you face north.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;saddle&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>On the saddle x² − y², walking east (x direction) the ground rises away from the centre, while walking north it falls: the two partial derivatives have opposite signs.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"estimating-partials-from-data\" data-h=\"3\">Estimating partials from data</h3>\n<p>If you only have a table of values, partial derivatives can be estimated with difference quotients in one variable at a time. Suppose <span class=\"m\">f(2, 1) = 10</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(2.1, 1) = 10.6</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(2, 1.1) = 9.8</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">f_x(2, 1) \\approx \\frac{10.6 - 10}{0.1} = 6</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y(2, 1) \\approx \\frac{9.8 - 10}{0.1} = -2</span>. This is how gradient-checking code tests a neural network's backpropagation: nudge one weight, measure the loss change, and compare.</p>\n<h3 id=\"higher-order-partial-derivatives\" data-h=\"3\">Higher-order partial derivatives</h3>\n<p>Partial derivatives are themselves functions, so we can differentiate them again. There are four second-order partials of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_{xx}</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy}</span>, and the <strong>mixed partials</strong> <span class=\"m\">f_{xy}</span> (first <span class=\"m\">x</span>, then <span class=\"m\">y</span>) and <span class=\"m\">f_{yx}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2y + 3y^3</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 2y</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = 18y</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}(2xy) = 2x</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{yx} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial x}(x^2 + 9y^2) = 2x</span>. The mixed partials agree. That is not luck: <strong>Clairaut's theorem</strong> says they are equal whenever they are continuous, which is true for essentially every function you will meet.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Mixed partial derivative</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A second-order partial taken with respect to two different variables, such as <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\right)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<p><span class=\"m\">f_{xx}</span> measures the curvature of the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-slice, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy}</span> the curvature of the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-slice, and the mixed partial measures how the <span class=\"m\">x</span>-slope changes as you move in <span class=\"m\">y</span>, a twisting of the surface. For the saddle <span class=\"m\">x^2 - y^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 2 &gt; 0</span> (curving up) and <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = -2 &lt; 0</span> (curving down). These second partials will form the Hessian matrix used to classify critical points.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The classic error is forgetting to treat the other variable as a constant: differentiating <span class=\"m\">x^2y</span> with respect to <span class=\"m\">x</span> and writing <span class=\"m\">2xy + x^2</span> as if it were a product of two functions of <span class=\"m\">x</span>. A second is confusing the order in mixed partial notation; fortunately Clairaut usually makes them equal. A third is believing partial derivatives tell the whole story: they only describe the coordinate directions, and the next lessons show how to combine them for any direction.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A partial derivative measures change in one input direction with the others held constant; compute it by treating other variables as constants. Geometrically it is the slope of a slice of the surface. Second partials measure curvature and twisting, and by Clairaut's theorem the mixed partials are equal for smooth functions.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-surface\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;slices&quot;, &quot;x0&quot;: 1, &quot;y0&quot;: 0.5}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The pink slice holds y fixed and the orange slice holds x fixed. Their slopes at the point are the two partial derivatives.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the bowl <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0.5)</span>, read <span class=\"m\">f_x</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y</span> from the panel. Check them against <span class=\"m\">f_x = 2x</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = 2y</span>.\n2. Move <span class=\"m\">x_0</span> to 0 and watch the pink slice's slope become 0.\n3. Switch to <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span> and set the point to <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>.\n4. Switch to the mixed surface <span class=\"m\">x^3 - 3x + y^2</span> and find a point where both slopes are 0.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> each slice is an ordinary one-variable curve, and its slope at the point is a partial derivative, just as in the article's hiker analogy. For <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_x = y</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y = x</span>, so swapping the point swaps the two slopes. On the mixed surface both partials vanish at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span>: these are critical points, previewing optimization.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">f_x(a, b) = \\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a + h, b) - f(a, b)}{h} \\qquad f_y(a, b) = \\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a, b + h) - f(a, b)}{h}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f_x \\approx \\frac{f(x + h, y) - f(x, y)}{h} \\qquad f_x \\approx \\frac{f(x + h, y) - f(x - h, y)}{2h}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f_{xx} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial x^2} \\qquad f_{xy} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial y\\,\\partial x}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f_{xy} = f_{yx} \\text{ (Clairaut, when continuous)}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Clairaut's theorem (symmetry of mixed partials)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f_{xy}</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{yx}</span> are both continuous on an open set containing <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">f_{xy}(a, b) = f_{yx}(a, b)</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Find all first partials of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y, z) = x^2z + yz^3 - 4xy</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_x</span>: treat <span class=\"m\">y, z</span> as constants: <span class=\"m\">2xz - 4y</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_y</span>: <span class=\"m\">z^3 - 4x</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_z</span>: <span class=\"m\">x^2 + 3yz^2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Partials of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = \\sin(x^2y)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Chain rule with inside <span class=\"m\">u = x^2y</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_x = \\cos(x^2y)\\cdot2xy</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_y = \\cos(x^2y)\\cdot x^2</span>.</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_x = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = 1</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Verify Clairaut for <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^3y^2 + e^{2x}y</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_x = 3x^2y^2 + 2e^{2x}y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = 6x^2y + 2e^{2x}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_y = 2x^3y + e^{2x}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f_{yx} = 6x^2y + 2e^{2x}</span>.</li>\n<li>They agree, as the theorem predicts.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The production of a factory is modelled by the Cobb–Douglas function <span class=\"m\">P(L, K) = 10L^{0.5}K^{0.5}</span>, with labour <span class=\"m\">L</span> and capital <span class=\"m\">K</span>. At <span class=\"m\">L = 25</span>, <span class=\"m\">K = 100</span>: (a) find <span class=\"m\">P</span>; (b) find <span class=\"m\">P_L</span> and <span class=\"m\">P_K</span> and interpret them; (c) which single extra unit, labour or capital, increases production more?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">P = 10\\cdot5\\cdot10 = 500</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">P_L = 5L^{-0.5}K^{0.5} = 5\\cdot\\frac15\\cdot10 = 10</span>: one more unit of labour adds about 10 units of output, holding capital fixed. <span class=\"m\">P_K = 5L^{0.5}K^{-0.5} = 5\\cdot5\\cdot\\frac{1}{10} = 2.5</span>. (c) Labour: its marginal product is 10, versus 2.5 for capital.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">One partial per weight</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Training computes <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w_i}</span> for every weight <span class=\"m\">w_i</span>: how much the loss changes if only that weight is nudged. In linear regression with loss <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac1n\\sum(w x_i + b - y_i)^2</span>, the partials are <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w} = \\frac2n\\sum(wx_i + b - y_i)x_i</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial b} = \\frac2n\\sum(wx_i + b - y_i)</span>. Numerical \"gradient checking\" estimates each partial with a centred difference to catch backpropagation bugs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Finite differences and sensitivity analysis</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Engineers and simulation software estimate partial derivatives by rerunning a model with one input perturbed. The centred difference <span class=\"m\">\\frac{f(x + h) - f(x - h)}{2h}</span> has error proportional to <span class=\"m\">h^2</span>, but making <span class=\"m\">h</span> too small amplifies floating-point rounding error, a classic numerical trade-off.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">The heat index</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>At 32°C and 70% humidity the heat index is roughly 41°C. Tables show that at these conditions one extra degree of temperature raises the felt temperature by about 2°C, while one extra percent of humidity adds about 0.3°C. Those are estimates of the partial derivatives <span class=\"m\">H_T \\approx 2</span> and <span class=\"m\">H_h \\approx 0.3</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^3 + xy^2</span>, then <span class=\"m\">f_x</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">3x^2 + y^2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">3x^2 + 2xy</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">2xy</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Treat <span class=\"m\">y</span> as a constant.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2y</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">f_y(3, 5)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_y = x^2 = 9</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = e^{2x + 3y}</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">f_y(0, 0)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Chain rule: <span class=\"m\">3e^{2x + 3y}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2y^3</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xy}</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">6xy^2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">2xy^3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">6x^2y</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_x = 2xy^3</span>, then differentiate in <span class=\"m\">y</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">f(1, 2) = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">f(1.01, 2) = 5.03</span>, estimate <span class=\"m\">f_x(1, 2)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{0.03}{0.01}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 - y^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy}</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_y = -2y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = -2</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "tangent-planes": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"zooming-in-on-a-surface\" data-h=\"3\">Zooming in on a surface</h3>\n<p>In single-variable calculus, zooming in on a smooth curve makes it look like a straight line, the tangent line. The same thing happens with surfaces: zoom in far enough on a smooth surface and it looks flat, like a tilted plane. That plane is the <strong>tangent plane</strong>. It lets us approximate a complicated function of several variables by a simple linear one, which is the foundation of gradient descent, error estimates, and nearly every algorithm that \"takes a small step\".</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>The Earth is round, but a football pitch looks perfectly flat. On a small enough patch, a curved surface is indistinguishable from a plane, and surveyors happily use flat-map geometry for a building site. In the same spirit, if you know a function's value and its two partial derivatives at a point, you can predict its values nearby without evaluating the complicated formula.</p>\n<h3 id=\"building-the-tangent-plane\" data-h=\"3\">Building the tangent plane</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> and pick a point <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span> with height <span class=\"m\">c = f(a, b)</span>. A non-vertical plane through <span class=\"m\">(a, b, c)</span> has the form <span class=\"m\">z = c + m(x - a) + n(y - b)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">m</span> is its slope in the <span class=\"m\">x</span> direction and <span class=\"m\">n</span> its slope in the <span class=\"m\">y</span> direction. For the plane to be tangent, these slopes must match the slopes of the surface's slices, which are the partial derivatives. So\n<span class=\"mb\">z = f(a, b) + f_x(a, b)(x - a) + f_y(a, b)(y - b).</span>\nThat's it: one point and two slopes determine the plane, just as one point and one slope determine a tangent line.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Tangent plane</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For differentiable <span class=\"m\">f</span>, the plane <span class=\"m\">z = f(a, b) + f_x(a, b)(x - a) + f_y(a, b)(y - b)</span>, which touches the surface <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> at <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span> and matches both partial slopes there.</p></div></div>\n<p>Example: <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">f = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_x = 2x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = 2y = 4</span>. The tangent plane is <span class=\"m\">z = 5 + 2(x - 1) + 4(y - 2)</span>, which simplifies to <span class=\"m\">z = 2x + 4y - 5</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Near any point, the bowl's contours look like nearly straight, parallel lines: zoomed in, the surface is almost a plane.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"linear-approximation\" data-h=\"3\">Linear approximation</h3>\n<p>The tangent plane, viewed as a function, is the <strong>linearization</strong> of <span class=\"m\">f</span> at <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">L(x, y) = f(a, b) + f_x(a, b)(x - a) + f_y(a, b)(y - b),</span>\nand near <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) \\approx L(x, y)</span>. For the bowl at <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>: estimate <span class=\"m\">f(1.1, 1.9)</span>. <span class=\"m\">L = 5 + 2(0.1) + 4(-0.1) = 4.8</span>. The exact value is <span class=\"m\">1.21 + 3.61 = 4.82</span>. The error, <span class=\"m\">0.02</span>, comes from the bowl's curvature and shrinks like the square of the distance.</p>\n<p>In vector form, with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = (x, y)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a = (a, b)</span>, the linearization is <span class=\"m\">L(\\mathbf x) = f(\\mathbf a) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\cdot(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (f_x, f_y)</span> is the gradient, the subject of the next lesson. This form works in any number of dimensions.</p>\n<h3 id=\"differentials-and-error-propagation\" data-h=\"3\">Differentials and error propagation</h3>\n<p>Write small changes as <span class=\"m\">dx</span> and <span class=\"m\">dy</span>. The linearization says the change in <span class=\"m\">f</span> is about\n<span class=\"mb\">df = f_x\\,dx + f_y\\,dy.</span>\nThis <strong>total differential</strong> shows how errors in several measurements combine. A rectangle is measured as 10 cm by 6 cm, each with error up to 0.1 cm. Its area <span class=\"m\">A = xy</span> has <span class=\"m\">dA = y\\,dx + x\\,dy = 6(0.1) + 10(0.1) = 1.6</span> cm², so the area <span class=\"m\">60</span> cm² is accurate to within about <span class=\"m\">1.6</span> cm². Notice that the error in the <em>shorter</em> side is multiplied by the <em>longer</em> side: the area is more sensitive to it.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Total differential</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">df = f_x\\,dx + f_y\\,dy</span> (more generally <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i f_{x_i}dx_i</span>), the linear estimate of the change in <span class=\"m\">f</span> caused by small input changes.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"differentiability\" data-h=\"3\">Differentiability</h3>\n<p>In one variable, having a derivative is enough for a tangent line to exist. In several variables, having partial derivatives is <em>not</em> enough: the partials only look along the two axis directions. A function is <strong>differentiable</strong> at a point if the tangent plane approximation is good in every direction, meaning the error <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) - L(\\mathbf x)</span> shrinks faster than the distance <span class=\"m\">\\|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a\\|</span>. A convenient sufficient condition: if the partial derivatives exist near the point and are continuous there, <span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable. Polynomials, exponentials, and the smooth activation functions of neural networks all satisfy this. The function <span class=\"m\">\\frac{xy}{x^2 + y^2}</span> (with value 0 at the origin) is a warning example: both its partials at the origin are 0, yet it is not even continuous there.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Differentiable (several variables)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span> if <span class=\"m\">\\lim_{\\mathbf x\\to\\mathbf a}\\frac{f(\\mathbf x) - f(\\mathbf a) - \\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\cdot(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)}{\\|\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a\\|} = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"tangent-planes-to-level-surfaces\" data-h=\"3\">Tangent planes to level surfaces</h3>\n<p>Surfaces are sometimes given implicitly, like the sphere <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 14</span>, as a level surface <span class=\"m\">F(x, y, z) = c</span>. Then the tangent plane at <span class=\"m\">(a, b, c_0)</span> is <span class=\"m\">F_x(x - a) + F_y(y - b) + F_z(z - c_0) = 0</span>, because the gradient of <span class=\"m\">F</span> is perpendicular to its level surfaces. For the sphere at <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3)</span>: <span class=\"m\">F_x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">F_y = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">F_z = 6</span>, so the plane is <span class=\"m\">2(x - 1) + 4(y - 2) + 6(z - 3) = 0</span>, or <span class=\"m\">x + 2y + 3z = 14</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Forgetting to evaluate the partial derivatives at the point (leaving <span class=\"m\">2x</span> instead of 2) gives a curved \"plane\". Using the linearization far from the point gives poor estimates, just as in one variable. And do not assume differentiability from the existence of partials alone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The tangent plane <span class=\"m\">z = f(a, b) + f_x(a, b)(x - a) + f_y(a, b)(y - b)</span> is the flat surface that best matches <span class=\"m\">f</span> near <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>. As a function it is the linearization, giving estimates <span class=\"m\">f \\approx L</span> and differentials <span class=\"m\">df = f_xdx + f_ydy</span> for error propagation. Differentiable functions are exactly those that look flat when you zoom in.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-surface\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;plane&quot;, &quot;x0&quot;: 1, &quot;y0&quot;: 0.5}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The translucent plane touches the surface at the chosen point. Rotate the view and move the point.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the bowl at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0.5)</span>, read the tangent plane equation in the panel. Check it: <span class=\"m\">f = 1.25</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_x = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = 1</span>.\n2. Rotate the view to look along the plane's edge and see how it touches the bowl only at one point.\n3. Move the point to <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>.\n4. Switch to the saddle and place the point at <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, then at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> near the point the plane and surface nearly coincide, which is why <span class=\"m\">L(1.1, 1.9) = 4.8</span> is so close to the true <span class=\"m\">4.82</span> in the article. At the bottom of the bowl the plane is horizontal, because both partials are 0. At the saddle's centre the plane is horizontal too, yet the surface lies above it in one direction and below it in another: a flat tangent plane does not guarantee a minimum.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">z = f(a, b) + f_x(a, b)(x - a) + f_y(a, b)(y - b)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(\\mathbf x) = f(\\mathbf a) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\cdot(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">df = f_x\\,dx + f_y\\,dy \\qquad \\Delta f \\approx df</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(x, y, z) = c:\\ F_x(a, b, c_0)(x - a) + F_y(\\cdot)(y - b) + F_z(\\cdot)(z - c_0) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dA}{A} = \\frac{dx}{x} + \\frac{dy}{y} \\text{ for } A = xy</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Continuous partials imply differentiability</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f_x</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y</span> exist in a neighbourhood of <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span> and are continuous at <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span>, and the tangent plane approximation has error <span class=\"m\">o(\\|(x - a, y - b)\\|)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Write <span class=\"m\">f(a + h, b + k) - f(a, b) = [f(a + h, b + k) - f(a, b + k)] + [f(a, b + k) - f(a, b)]</span>. By the one-variable mean value theorem, the first bracket is <span class=\"m\">f_x(a + \\theta h, b + k)h</span> and the second is <span class=\"m\">f_y(a, b + \\phi k)k</span> for some <span class=\"m\">\\theta, \\phi \\in (0, 1)</span>. By continuity these partials differ from <span class=\"m\">f_x(a, b)</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y(a, b)</span> by amounts that tend to 0, so the error <span class=\"m\">f - L</span> is (something tending to 0) times <span class=\"m\">|h| + |k|</span>, which is small compared with <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{h^2 + k^2}</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Tangent plane to <span class=\"m\">z = e^{x}\\cos y</span> at <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(0, 0) = 1</span>; <span class=\"m\">f_x = e^x\\cos y = 1</span>; <span class=\"m\">f_y = -e^x\\sin y = 0</span> at the origin.</li>\n<li>Plane: <span class=\"m\">z = 1 + 1(x - 0) + 0(y - 0)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">z = 1 + x</span>. So <span class=\"m\">e^{0.1}\\cos(0.05) \\approx 1.1</span> (true value <span class=\"m\">1.1038</span>).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Estimate <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{3.02^2 + 3.97^2}</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}</span> at <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>: <span class=\"m\">f = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_x = \\frac{x}{f} = 0.6</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = \\frac{y}{f} = 0.8</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">L = 5 + 0.6(0.02) + 0.8(-0.03) = 5 + 0.012 - 0.024</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\approx 4.988</span> (true value <span class=\"m\">4.98804</span>).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Error in a cylinder's volume</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">V = \\pi r^2h</span> with <span class=\"m\">r = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">h = 10</span>, each measured to <span class=\"m\">\\pm0.1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">dV = 2\\pi rh\\,dr + \\pi r^2\\,dh = 2\\pi(50)(0.1) + \\pi(25)(0.1) = 10\\pi + 2.5\\pi</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">dV = 12.5\\pi \\approx 39.3</span>, compared with <span class=\"m\">V = 250\\pi \\approx 785</span>: about a 5% error.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2y - y^3</span>. (a) Find the tangent plane at <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>. (b) Use it to estimate <span class=\"m\">f(2.1, 0.9)</span> and compare with the exact value. (c) Find the tangent plane to the level surface <span class=\"m\">xyz = 6</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 2, 3)</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">f(2, 1) = 4 - 1 = 3</span>; <span class=\"m\">f_x = 2xy = 4</span>; <span class=\"m\">f_y = x^2 - 3y^2 = 1</span>. Plane: <span class=\"m\">z = 3 + 4(x - 2) + (y - 1)</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">L = 3 + 0.4 - 0.1 = 3.3</span>; exact <span class=\"m\">4.41(0.9) - 0.729 = 3.969 - 0.729 = 3.24</span>, error <span class=\"m\">0.06</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">F = xyz</span>: <span class=\"m\">F_x = yz = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">F_y = xz = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">F_z = xy = 2</span>. Plane: <span class=\"m\">6(x - 1) + 3(y - 2) + 2(z - 3) = 0</span>, i.e. <span class=\"m\">6x + 3y + 2z = 18</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">First-order approximations in training</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every gradient step relies on <span class=\"m\">L(\\mathbf w + \\Delta) \\approx L(\\mathbf w) + \\nabla L\\cdot\\Delta</span>, the tangent-plane approximation of the loss. Choosing <span class=\"m\">\\Delta = -\\eta\\nabla L</span> makes the predicted change <span class=\"m\">-\\eta\\|\\nabla L\\|^2 &lt; 0</span>. The approximation is only trustworthy for small steps, which is why learning rates are small, and trust-region methods explicitly limit the step to the region where the linear (or quadratic) model is accurate. Explanation methods like gradient × input also use the linearization of a network around an input.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Linearization in graphics and robotics</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Robots and self-driving cars track their position with the extended Kalman filter, which linearizes nonlinear motion and sensor models around the current estimate using exactly these tangent-plane formulas. Shading in computer graphics uses surface normals, the vectors perpendicular to tangent planes, to compute how light reflects.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Measurement error in engineering</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>An engineer computes a beam's stress from several measured quantities. The total differential tells her which measurement's error matters most: if stress is proportional to <span class=\"m\">\\frac{F}{d^2}</span>, a 1% error in the diameter <span class=\"m\">d</span> causes a 2% error in stress, twice the effect of a 1% error in the force <span class=\"m\">F</span>. She spends her budget on a better caliper.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The tangent plane to <span class=\"m\">z = x^2 + y^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> is <span class=\"m\">z = 2 + 2(x - 1) + b(y - 1)</span>. What is <span class=\"m\">b</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_y = 2y = 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Using the tangent plane of <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span> at <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span>, estimate <span class=\"m\">f(2.1, 3)</span>.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6.3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">L = 6 + 3(0.1) + 2(0) = 6.3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The total differential of <span class=\"m\">f = x^2y</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">2xy\\,dx + x^2\\,dy</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">2x\\,dx + dy</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">2xy\\,dx\\,dy</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_xdx + f_ydy</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A tangent plane at a minimum of a smooth function is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">horizontal</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">vertical</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">undefined</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Both partials are 0.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A rectangle is 4 by 5 and each side grows by 0.1. Estimate the change in area.</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"0.9\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">dA = 5(0.1) + 4(0.1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Existence of both partial derivatives at a point guarantees differentiability.</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">False</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">True</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Continuity of the partials is the usual sufficient condition.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "gradient": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"which-way-is-uphill\" data-h=\"3\">Which way is uphill?</h3>\n<p>Partial derivatives tell you how steep a hill is if you walk due east or due north. But you can walk in any direction. Which direction is steepest, and how steep is it? The answer is a single vector built from the partials: the <strong>gradient</strong>. It points straight uphill, its length tells you how steep, and its opposite direction is the route gradient descent takes to train nearly every machine learning model. If one idea from multivariable calculus matters most for ML, it is this one.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>You are standing on a foggy hillside and want to reach the valley floor. You cannot see far, but you can feel the slope under your feet. A sensible plan: feel which direction slopes down most steeply, take a step that way, and repeat. That is gradient descent. To make it work, you need to know, at each spot, the direction of steepest descent, which is minus the gradient.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-gradient-vector\" data-h=\"3\">The gradient vector</h3>\n<p>The gradient of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> is the vector of its partial derivatives:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f(x, y) = \\big(f_x(x, y),\\ f_y(x, y)\\big).</span>\nThe symbol <span class=\"m\">\\nabla</span> is called \"nabla\" or \"del\". In <span class=\"m\">n</span> variables, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (f_{x_1}, \\ldots, f_{x_n})</span>. For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + 3y^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2x, 6y)</span>, so at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(2, 6)</span>. The gradient is a vector field: at every point of the plane it gives an arrow.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1}, \\ldots, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_n}\\right)</span>, the vector of all first partial derivatives of <span class=\"m\">f</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"directional-derivatives\" data-h=\"3\">Directional derivatives</h3>\n<p>Suppose you walk from <span class=\"m\">(a, b)</span> in the direction of a <strong>unit</strong> vector <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = (u_1, u_2)</span>. The rate at which <span class=\"m\">f</span> changes is the <strong>directional derivative</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{\\mathbf u}f(a, b) = \\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a + hu_1, b + hu_2) - f(a, b)}{h}.</span>\nUsing the tangent plane, moving a small distance <span class=\"m\">h</span> along <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> changes <span class=\"m\">f</span> by about <span class=\"m\">f_x(hu_1) + f_y(hu_2)</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">h</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{\\mathbf u}f = f_xu_1 + f_yu_2 = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u.</span>\nThe partial derivatives are just the special cases <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = (1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>. Example: for <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 3y^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> in the direction of <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, first normalize: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = \\left(\\frac35, \\frac45\\right)</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">D_{\\mathbf u}f = 2\\cdot\\frac35 + 6\\cdot\\frac45 = \\frac{6 + 24}{5} = 6</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Directional derivative</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">D_{\\mathbf u}f(\\mathbf a) = \\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\cdot\\mathbf u</span> for a unit vector <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span>: the rate of change of <span class=\"m\">f</span> per unit distance moved in direction <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"why-the-gradient-points-uphill\" data-h=\"3\">Why the gradient points uphill</h3>\n<p>The dot product formula <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u = \\|\\nabla f\\|\\,\\|\\mathbf u\\|\\cos\\theta = \\|\\nabla f\\|\\cos\\theta</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> is the angle between <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> and the gradient. This is largest when <span class=\"m\">\\cos\\theta = 1</span>, that is when <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> points the same way as <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span>. So:</p>\n<p>The gradient points in the direction of <strong>steepest ascent</strong>, and the steepest rate of increase is <span class=\"m\">\\|\\nabla f\\|</span>. The opposite direction, <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f</span>, is the direction of <strong>steepest descent</strong>, with rate <span class=\"m\">-\\|\\nabla f\\|</span>. Directions perpendicular to the gradient (<span class=\"m\">\\theta = 90°</span>) have rate 0: walking that way keeps you at the same height, along a contour.</p>\n<p>That last fact gives a beautiful geometric picture: <strong>the gradient is perpendicular to the level curves.</strong> On a contour map, gradient arrows cross the contours at right angles, pointing toward higher values, and they are longest where contours are closest together. For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 3y^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>, the steepest rate is <span class=\"m\">\\|(2, 6)\\| = \\sqrt{40} \\approx 6.32</span>, a little more than the 6 we found in direction <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;ravine&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Gradient arrows cross the contours at right angles and point uphill; the negative gradient points downhill.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"gradient-descent\" data-h=\"3\">Gradient descent</h3>\n<p>To minimize a function, repeatedly step in the steepest-descent direction:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf x_{k+1} = \\mathbf x_k - \\eta\\,\\nabla f(\\mathbf x_k),</span>\nwhere the <strong>learning rate</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\eta &gt; 0</span> controls the step size. For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 3y^2</span> starting at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.1</span>: the gradient is <span class=\"m\">(2, 6)</span>, so the next point is <span class=\"m\">(1 - 0.2,\\ 1 - 0.6) = (0.8, 0.4)</span>, and <span class=\"m\">f</span> drops from 4 to <span class=\"m\">0.64 + 0.48 = 1.12</span>. Repeating drives the point to the minimum at the origin. If <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span> is too large (here, above <span class=\"m\">\\frac13</span>, since the <span class=\"m\">y</span>-curvature is 6), the steps overshoot and diverge. On elongated valleys like this one, the gradient points mostly across the valley rather than along it, so plain gradient descent zig-zags; momentum and Adam were invented to fix that.</p>\n<h3 id=\"gradients-of-common-functions\" data-h=\"3\">Gradients of common functions</h3>\n<p>A few gradients come up so often in ML that they are worth knowing by heart. For a linear function <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x</span>, the gradient is simply <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w</span>, the same at every point, which is why a linear model's contours are parallel lines. For the squared length <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = \\|\\mathbf x\\|^2 = x_1^2 + \\cdots + x_n^2</span>, each partial is <span class=\"m\">2x_i</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = 2\\mathbf x</span>, pointing straight away from the origin. Gradients also obey sum and product rules exactly like derivatives, so complicated losses can be handled piece by piece.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Forgetting to normalize the direction vector is the most common error in directional derivatives: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f\\cdot(3, 4)</span> is five times too big. Another is thinking the gradient points toward the maximum; it points in the locally steepest direction, which can be quite different. Finally, remember the gradient lives in the input space (the <span class=\"m\">xy</span>-plane), not on the surface itself.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The gradient <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> collects all partial derivatives into a vector. The rate of change in a unit direction <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u</span>. The gradient points in the direction of steepest ascent, with rate <span class=\"m\">\\|\\nabla f\\|</span>, and is perpendicular to level curves. Gradient descent steps along <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f</span> with learning rate <span class=\"m\">\\eta</span>.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;ravine&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the point. Pink = gradient (uphill), green = negative gradient (the way gradient descent goes).</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Drag the point around the ravine and watch the arrows relative to the contour lines.\n2. Move the point to where contours are tightly packed, then to where they are sparse.\n3. Switch to the bowl and the saddle.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the arrows always cross the contours at right angles, as the article proved. They are long where contours are packed (steep) and short where they are sparse. In the ravine, the green arrow points mostly across the valley, not along it toward the minimum, which explains the zig-zagging of plain gradient descent.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.1}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Press Run. Try a large learning rate and watch it overshoot or diverge.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With learning rate <span class=\"m\">0.1</span> on the bowl, press Run and count how quickly the path reaches the centre.\n2. Raise the learning rate step by step toward <span class=\"m\">0.6</span>.\n3. Switch to the ravine with plain GD, then compare Momentum and Adam.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> small learning rates converge smoothly but slowly; larger ones get there faster until they start to overshoot, bouncing across the bowl, and eventually diverge. On the ravine, plain GD zig-zags across the narrow direction, while momentum builds up speed along the valley floor.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = (f_x, f_y) \\qquad \\nabla f = \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1}, \\ldots, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_n}\\right)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{\\mathbf u}f = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u = \\|\\nabla f\\|\\cos\\theta\\quad(\\|\\mathbf u\\| = 1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\max_{\\mathbf u}D_{\\mathbf u}f = \\|\\nabla f\\| \\text{ in direction } \\frac{\\nabla f}{\\|\\nabla f\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf x_{k+1} = \\mathbf x_k - \\eta\\,\\nabla f(\\mathbf x_k)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla(f + g) = \\nabla f + \\nabla g \\qquad \\nabla(fg) = g\\nabla f + f\\nabla g \\qquad \\nabla(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf w \\qquad \\nabla\\|\\mathbf x\\|^2 = 2\\mathbf x</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient is the direction of steepest ascent</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a) \\ne \\mathbf 0</span>, then among all unit vectors <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span>, <span class=\"m\">D_{\\mathbf u}f(\\mathbf a)</span> is largest for <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = \\frac{\\nabla f(\\mathbf a)}{\\|\\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\|}</span>, with maximum value <span class=\"m\">\\|\\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\|</span>; it is zero for <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> perpendicular to the gradient.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">D_{\\mathbf u}f = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u</span>. By the Cauchy–Schwarz inequality, <span class=\"m\">|\\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u| \\le \\|\\nabla f\\|\\|\\mathbf u\\| = \\|\\nabla f\\|</span>, with equality exactly when <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u</span> is a positive multiple of <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> (for the maximum). When <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u \\perp \\nabla f</span>, the dot product is 0.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient is perpendicular to level sets</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r(t)</span> is a curve lying in the level set <span class=\"m\">f = c</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t) = 0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Directional derivative of <span class=\"m\">f = xy^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span> toward <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (y^2, 2xy) = (1, 4)</span>.</li>\n<li>Unit direction: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(1, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">D_{\\mathbf u}f = \\frac{1 + 4}{\\sqrt2} = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt2} \\approx 3.54</span>; the maximum possible is <span class=\"m\">\\|(1, 4)\\| = \\sqrt{17} \\approx 4.12</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Steepest descent direction for <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + xy + y^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2x + y, x + 2y) = (4, 5)</span>.</li>\n<li>Steepest descent: <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f = (-4, -5)</span>; unit vector <span class=\"m\">\\frac{(-4, -5)}{\\sqrt{41}}</span>.</li>\n<li>Rate of decrease: <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{41} \\approx 6.40</span> per unit distance.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Two gradient descent steps on <span class=\"m\">f = (x - 3)^2 + 2y^2</span> from <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.25</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2(x - 3), 4y) = (-6, 4)</span>; step: <span class=\"m\">(0, 1) - 0.25(-6, 4) = (1.5, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(1.5, 0) = (-3, 0)</span>; step: <span class=\"m\">(1.5 + 0.75, 0) = (2.25, 0)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f</span> falls from <span class=\"m\">11</span> to <span class=\"m\">2.25</span> to <span class=\"m\">0.5625</span>, heading to the minimum at <span class=\"m\">(3, 0)</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The temperature on a metal plate is <span class=\"m\">T(x, y) = 100 - x^2 - 2y^2</span>. An ant is at <span class=\"m\">(2, 1)</span>. (a) In which unit direction should it move to warm up fastest, and at what rate? (b) What is the rate of change if it moves toward the point <span class=\"m\">(5, 5)</span>? (c) In which directions does the temperature not change?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\nabla T = (-2x, -4y) = (-4, -4)</span>. Fastest warming: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(-1, -1)</span>, at rate <span class=\"m\">\\|\\nabla T\\| = 4\\sqrt2 \\approx 5.66</span> degrees per unit. (b) Direction <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>, unit <span class=\"m\">\\left(\\frac35, \\frac45\\right)</span>: <span class=\"m\">D_{\\mathbf u}T = -4\\cdot\\frac35 - 4\\cdot\\frac45 = -\\frac{28}{5} = -5.6</span>, cooling. (c) Perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">(-4, -4)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt2}(1, -1)</span>, along the isotherm.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient descent and its variants</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Neural networks are trained by stochastic gradient descent: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w \\leftarrow \\mathbf w - \\eta\\nabla L_{\\text{batch}}(\\mathbf w)</span>, where the gradient comes from backpropagation. Momentum averages past gradients to damp zig-zagging; Adam rescales each coordinate by a running estimate of its gradient size. Gradients with respect to inputs are used too: saliency maps show which pixels most affect a prediction, and adversarial examples step along <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{sign}(\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}L)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Edge detection</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In image processing, the gradient of pixel intensity points across edges. The Sobel operator estimates <span class=\"m\">\\nabla I</span> with small finite-difference filters; its magnitude highlights edges and its direction gives their orientation, the first stage of the Canny edge detector.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Water flowing downhill</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Rain on a hillside flows in the direction of <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla h</span>, the steepest descent of the terrain height. Hydrologists compute drainage networks and flood paths from digital elevation maps by following negative gradients from every cell.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + y^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(3, 4)</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">(6, 8)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">(9, 16)</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">(2x, 2y)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The maximum rate of increase of <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + y^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"10\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\|(6, 8)\\| = 10</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2, 6)</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">D_{\\mathbf u}f</span> in the unit direction <span class=\"m\">(0.6, 0.8)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1.2 + 4.8</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The gradient at a point is ... to the level curve through that point.</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">perpendicular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">parallel</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">tangent</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Moving along a contour gives zero change.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">One gradient step on <span class=\"m\">f = x^2</span> from <span class=\"m\">x = 3</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\eta = 0.25</span> lands at...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1.5|3/2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">3 - 0.25\\cdot6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Gradient descent moves in the direction of...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">-\\nabla f</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a contour line</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Steepest descent.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "multi-chain": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"chains-with-several-links\" data-h=\"3\">Chains with several links</h3>\n<p>In single-variable calculus, the chain rule says rates multiply along a chain: if <span class=\"m\">y</span> depends on <span class=\"m\">u</span> and <span class=\"m\">u</span> on <span class=\"m\">x</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{dy}{du}\\frac{du}{dx}</span>. In several variables, a quantity can depend on its inputs through <strong>several paths at once</strong>. The temperature you feel on a car trip depends on your position <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span>, and both coordinates change with time. A neural network's loss depends on a weight through every neuron that weight influences. The multivariable chain rule says: multiply rates along each path, then <strong>add over all paths</strong>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>You are driving through hilly country. The height of the road is <span class=\"m\">h(x, y)</span> and your position changes with time, <span class=\"m\">x(t)</span> and <span class=\"m\">y(t)</span>. How fast is your altitude changing? Part of the change comes from moving east (<span class=\"m\">h_x\\cdot\\frac{dx}{dt}</span>) and part from moving north (<span class=\"m\">h_y\\cdot\\frac{dy}{dt}</span>). The total rate is the sum. If the ground rises 0.1 m per metre eastward, you drive east at 20 m/s, it falls 0.05 m per metre northward and you drive north at 10 m/s, your altitude changes at <span class=\"m\">0.1(20) - 0.05(10) = 1.5</span> m/s.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-first-form-one-parameter\" data-h=\"3\">The first form: one parameter</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = x(t)</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = y(t)</span>, then\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\frac{dy}{dt}.</span>\nIn vector form this is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r(t) = (x(t), y(t))</span>: the gradient dotted with the velocity. That is exactly the directional derivative, scaled by speed.</p>\n<p>Example: <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2y</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = \\cos t</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = \\sin t</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">f_x = 2xy</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = x^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">x' = -\\sin t</span>, <span class=\"m\">y' = \\cos t</span>. At <span class=\"m\">t = 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = 0\\cdot0 + 1\\cdot1 = 1</span>. Check directly: <span class=\"m\">z = \\cos^2t\\sin t</span> has derivative <span class=\"m\">-2\\cos t\\sin^2t + \\cos^3t</span>, which is 1 at <span class=\"m\">t = 0</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Total derivative along a path</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">z = f(\\mathbf x(t))</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = \\sum_i\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_i}\\frac{dx_i}{dt} = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf x'(t)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-second-form-several-parameters\" data-h=\"3\">The second form: several parameters</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> where <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> both depend on <span class=\"m\">s</span> and <span class=\"m\">t</span>, then each partial derivative of <span class=\"m\">z</span> collects one term per path:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s} = \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}, \\qquad \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t} = \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}.</span>\nA <strong>tree diagram</strong> keeps this straight: draw <span class=\"m\">z</span> at the top, branches to <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>, and from each of those branches to <span class=\"m\">s</span> and <span class=\"m\">t</span>. Label each branch with its partial derivative. To get <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s}</span>, find every route from <span class=\"m\">z</span> down to <span class=\"m\">s</span>, multiply the labels along each route, and add the routes.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"func\" data-args='{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;linear&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Each link in a chain scales a small change by its own rate; with several paths, the contributions add.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Example: <span class=\"m\">z = x^2 + y^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = s + t</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = s - t</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s} = 2x\\cdot1 + 2y\\cdot1 = 2(s + t) + 2(s - t) = 4s</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">z = (s + t)^2 + (s - t)^2 = 2s^2 + 2t^2</span>, whose <span class=\"m\">s</span>-partial is <span class=\"m\">4s</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"why-it-works\" data-h=\"3\">Why it works</h3>\n<p>Nudge <span class=\"m\">t</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\Delta t</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">x</span> changes by about <span class=\"m\">x'\\Delta t</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> by about <span class=\"m\">y'\\Delta t</span>. By the tangent-plane approximation, <span class=\"m\">f</span> changes by about <span class=\"m\">f_x\\Delta x + f_y\\Delta y = (f_xx' + f_yy')\\Delta t</span>. Dividing by <span class=\"m\">\\Delta t</span> and letting it shrink gives the formula. The contributions add because, to first order, a smooth function behaves like a linear one, and linear effects simply add.</p>\n<h3 id=\"implicit-differentiation-revisited\" data-h=\"3\">Implicit differentiation revisited</h3>\n<p>The chain rule gives a slick formula for implicit curves. If <span class=\"m\">F(x, y) = 0</span> defines <span class=\"m\">y</span> as a function of <span class=\"m\">x</span>, differentiate both sides with respect to <span class=\"m\">x</span>: <span class=\"m\">F_x + F_y\\frac{dy}{dx} = 0</span>, so\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{F_x}{F_y}.</span>\nFor the circle <span class=\"m\">F = x^2 + y^2 - 25</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{2x}{2y} = -\\frac xy</span>, matching the single-variable lesson.</p>\n<h3 id=\"computational-graphs-and-backpropagation\" data-h=\"3\">Computational graphs and backpropagation</h3>\n<p>A neural network is a big composition. Write it as a <strong>computational graph</strong>: nodes are intermediate values, edges show which values feed which. The derivative of the output with respect to any node is the sum, over all paths from that node to the output, of the products of local derivatives along the path. Backpropagation computes all of these efficiently by working backward from the output: each node's gradient is the sum, over its children, of (child's gradient) × (local derivative). For example, with <span class=\"m\">L = (wx + b - y)^2</span>, define <span class=\"m\">u = wx + b - y</span>; then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w} = 2u\\cdot x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial b} = 2u\\cdot1</span>, sharing the factor <span class=\"m\">2u</span> computed once.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-chain-rule-in-matrix-form\" data-h=\"3\">The chain rule in matrix form</h3>\n<p>When there are many inputs and many intermediate variables, the sums become matrix products. If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = g(\\mathbf x)</span> and <span class=\"m\">z = f(\\mathbf u)</span>, collect the partials <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial u_i}{\\partial x_j}</span> into a matrix <span class=\"m\">J_g</span> (the Jacobian of the next lesson). Then <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}z = J_g^\\top\\nabla_{\\mathbf u}z</span>: each entry is a sum over paths through every <span class=\"m\">u_i</span>. For a linear layer <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = W\\mathbf x</span>, the Jacobian is <span class=\"m\">W</span> itself, so the gradient flowing back to the input is <span class=\"m\">W^\\top\\nabla_{\\mathbf u}z</span>. That single formula is how gradients pass backward through every dense layer of a neural network.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The most frequent error is missing a path, for instance forgetting that <span class=\"m\">y</span> also depends on <span class=\"m\">t</span>. Another is mixing up <span class=\"m\">\\frac{d}{dt}</span> (total, through all paths) and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}</span> (holding other variables fixed) when a function depends on <span class=\"m\">t</span> both directly and through other variables. Finally, evaluate every factor at the same point.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>When a variable influences the output through several intermediate variables, multiply derivatives along each path and add over paths: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = f_x x' + f_y y' = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf r'</span>. Tree diagrams organize the bookkeeping. Implicit derivatives follow as <span class=\"m\">-\\frac{F_x}{F_y}</span>. Backpropagation is this rule applied systematically to a computational graph.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-surface\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;dome&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;plane&quot;, &quot;x0&quot;: 1, &quot;y0&quot;: 0.5}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Moving the point changes height at a rate given by the tangent plane: dz = f_x dx + f_y dy, the chain rule's sum of paths.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. On the dome, read <span class=\"m\">f_x</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0.5)</span>: they should be <span class=\"m\">-2</span> and <span class=\"m\">-1</span>.\n2. Increase <span class=\"m\">x_0</span> by 0.1 and note the height change; then instead increase <span class=\"m\">y_0</span> by 0.1.\n3. Now increase both by 0.1 and compare with the sum of the two separate changes.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> moving 0.1 in <span class=\"m\">x</span> lowers the height by about <span class=\"m\">0.2</span>, moving 0.1 in <span class=\"m\">y</span> lowers it by about <span class=\"m\">0.1</span>, and moving in both lowers it by about <span class=\"m\">0.3</span>: contributions from each path add, exactly as <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = f_xx' + f_yy'</span> says. The small mismatch comes from curvature and disappears for smaller nudges.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s} = \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t_j} = \\sum_i\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x_i}\\frac{\\partial x_i}{\\partial t_j}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(x, y) = 0 \\Rightarrow \\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{F_x}{F_y}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial v} = \\sum_{c\\,\\in\\,\\text{children}(v)}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial c}\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Multivariable chain rule</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">x(t)</span> and <span class=\"m\">y(t)</span> are differentiable at <span class=\"m\">t</span> and <span class=\"m\">f</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">(x(t), y(t))</span>, then <span class=\"m\">z = f(x(t), y(t))</span> is differentiable and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = f_x\\,x'(t) + f_y\\,y'(t)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\Delta x</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\Delta y</span> be the changes produced by <span class=\"m\">\\Delta t</span>. Differentiability of <span class=\"m\">f</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\Delta z = f_x\\Delta x + f_y\\Delta y + \\varepsilon\\sqrt{\\Delta x^2 + \\Delta y^2}</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon\\to0</span> as <span class=\"m\">(\\Delta x, \\Delta y)\\to0</span>. Divide by <span class=\"m\">\\Delta t</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\Delta z}{\\Delta t} = f_x\\frac{\\Delta x}{\\Delta t} + f_y\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t} \\pm \\varepsilon\\sqrt{(\\Delta x/\\Delta t)^2 + (\\Delta y/\\Delta t)^2}</span>. As <span class=\"m\">\\Delta t\\to0</span>, the quotients tend to <span class=\"m\">x'</span> and <span class=\"m\">y'</span>, the square root stays bounded, and <span class=\"m\">\\varepsilon\\to0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\"><span class=\"m\">z = e^{xy}</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = t^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 3t</span>: find <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt}</span> at <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">z_x = ye^{xy}</span>, <span class=\"m\">z_y = xe^{xy}</span>; <span class=\"m\">x' = 2t</span>, <span class=\"m\">y' = 3</span>.</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span>: <span class=\"m\">x = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">e^{xy} = e^3</span>. So <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = 3e^3\\cdot2 + 1\\cdot e^3\\cdot3</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">= 9e^3 \\approx 180.8</span>. (Directly: <span class=\"m\">z = e^{3t^3}</span>, <span class=\"m\">z' = 9t^2e^{3t^3}</span>.)</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Polar coordinates: <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> with <span class=\"m\">x = r\\cos\\theta</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = r\\sin\\theta</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial r} = f_x\\cos\\theta + f_y\\sin\\theta</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial\\theta} = -f_xr\\sin\\theta + f_yr\\cos\\theta</span>.</li>\n<li>For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + y^2 = r^2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial r} = 2r\\cos^2\\theta + 2r\\sin^2\\theta = 2r</span>, as expected.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Implicit slope of <span class=\"m\">x^3 + y^3 = 6xy</span> at <span class=\"m\">(3, 3)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">F = x^3 + y^3 - 6xy</span>: <span class=\"m\">F_x = 3x^2 - 6y = 27 - 18 = 9</span>, <span class=\"m\">F_y = 3y^2 - 6x = 9</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{F_x}{F_y} = -1</span>.</li>\n<li>Check the point: <span class=\"m\">27 + 27 = 54 = 6\\cdot9</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A single neuron computes <span class=\"m\">z = w_1x_1 + w_2x_2 + b</span>, <span class=\"m\">a = \\sigma(z)</span> and loss <span class=\"m\">L = \\frac12(a - y)^2</span>. With <span class=\"m\">x = (1, 2)</span>, <span class=\"m\">w = (0.5, -0.25)</span>, <span class=\"m\">b = 0</span> and target <span class=\"m\">y = 1</span>, find <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w_1}</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w_2}</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial b}</span>. (Use <span class=\"m\">\\sigma(0) = 0.5</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\sigma' = \\sigma(1 - \\sigma)</span>.)</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Forward: <span class=\"m\">z = 0.5 - 0.5 + 0 = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">a = 0.5</span>, <span class=\"m\">L = 0.125</span>. Backward: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial a} = a - y = -0.5</span>; <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial a}{\\partial z} = 0.25</span>; so <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial z} = -0.125</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial w_1} = x_1 = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial w_2} = x_2 = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial b} = 1</span>. Results: <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w_1} = -0.125</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial w_2} = -0.25</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial b} = -0.125</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Backpropagation sums over paths</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In a network, a weight in an early layer affects the loss through every neuron downstream. The chain rule says its gradient is a sum over all those paths, which would be exponentially many to enumerate. Backprop avoids this by computing <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial v}</span> for each node once, from the output backward, and reusing it. When a value feeds several consumers (a residual connection, a shared embedding), its gradient is the sum of the gradients from each consumer, exactly the \"add over paths\" rule.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reverse-mode automatic differentiation</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Reverse-mode autodiff records a computational graph during the forward pass, then visits nodes in reverse topological order, accumulating adjoints <span class=\"m\">\\bar v = \\sum_c\\bar c\\,\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}</span>. Its cost is a small constant times the forward pass, regardless of the number of inputs, which is why it suits functions with millions of inputs and one scalar output.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Changing gas pressure</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For an ideal gas, <span class=\"m\">P = \\frac{kT}{V}</span>. If temperature rises at 2 K/min and volume grows at 0.5 L/min, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dP}{dt} = \\frac{k}{V}\\frac{dT}{dt} - \\frac{kT}{V^2}\\frac{dV}{dt}</span>: heating pushes pressure up, expansion pulls it down, and the chain rule weighs the two effects.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">z = xy</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = t</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = t^2</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dz}{dt}</span> at <span class=\"m\">t = 1</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">y\\cdot1 + x\\cdot2t = 1 + 2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The chain rule with several intermediate variables...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">adds the products along each path</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">multiplies all partial derivatives together</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">uses only the largest path</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Sum over paths, product along each.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">F(x, y) = x^2 + y^2 - 25</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">dy/dx</span> at <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-3/4|-0.75\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">-\\frac{F_x}{F_y} = -\\frac{6}{8}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2, -1)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r'(t) = (3, 4)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{d}{dt}f(\\mathbf r(t))</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">6 - 4</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">z = x^2 + y^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = s + t</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = s - t</span>. At <span class=\"m\">s = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">t = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\partial z/\\partial s</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">4s</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In backprop, a node used by two later nodes gets a gradient equal to...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the sum of the gradients from both</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the product of the two gradients</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the larger of the two</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Contributions from each path add.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "jacobian-hessian": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"organizing-many-derivatives\" data-h=\"3\">Organizing many derivatives</h3>\n<p>A function with many inputs has many partial derivatives, and a function with many outputs has even more. Writing them one by one quickly becomes unmanageable. Linear algebra gives us two tidy containers. The <strong>Jacobian</strong> collects all first partial derivatives of a vector-valued function into a matrix; it is the multivariable version of \"the derivative\". The <strong>Hessian</strong> collects all second partial derivatives of a scalar function; it is the multivariable version of \"the second derivative\" and describes curvature. Both appear constantly in ML: Jacobians in backpropagation and normalizing flows, Hessians in optimization and in understanding loss landscapes.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>A camera turns 3D positions into 2D pixel positions. If an object moves slightly, how do its pixel coordinates move? Each of the two outputs depends on each of the three inputs, so there are six rates of change. Arranged in a <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> grid, they form the Jacobian, and multiplying it by the small 3D movement gives the predicted 2D movement on the screen. Robot arms, graphics engines and tracking software use exactly this.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-jacobian-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The Jacobian matrix</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F : \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m</span> have components <span class=\"m\">F_1, \\ldots, F_m</span>. Its Jacobian is the <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix whose row <span class=\"m\">i</span> is the gradient of <span class=\"m\">F_i</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{\\mathbf F} = \\begin{pmatrix}\\frac{\\partial F_1}{\\partial x_1} &amp; \\cdots &amp; \\frac{\\partial F_1}{\\partial x_n}\\\\ \\vdots &amp; &amp; \\vdots\\\\ \\frac{\\partial F_m}{\\partial x_1} &amp; \\cdots &amp; \\frac{\\partial F_m}{\\partial x_n}\\end{pmatrix}.</span>\nRows correspond to outputs, columns to inputs. For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(x, y) = (x^2y,\\ 5x + \\sin y)</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">J = \\begin{pmatrix}2xy &amp; x^2\\\\ 5 &amp; \\cos y\\end{pmatrix}, \\qquad J(1, 0) = \\begin{pmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\\\ 5 &amp; 1\\end{pmatrix}.</span></p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobian matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F : \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R^m</span>, the <span class=\"m\">m\\times n</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">J</span> with entries <span class=\"m\">J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial F_i}{\\partial x_j}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"what-the-jacobian-means\" data-h=\"3\">What the Jacobian means</h3>\n<p>The Jacobian is the best linear approximation of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F</span> near a point: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) \\approx \\mathbf F(\\mathbf a) + J(\\mathbf a)\\mathbf h</span>. Near any point, a smooth map acts like multiplication by a matrix. With the example above at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>, a nudge <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h = (0.1, 0.2)</span> changes the output by about <span class=\"m\">J\\mathbf h = (0.2,\\ 0.7)</span>. Exactly: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(1.1, 0.2) - \\mathbf F(1, 0) = (0.242,\\ 0.699)</span>. Close.</p>\n<p>For a square Jacobian (<span class=\"m\">n = m</span>), the <strong>determinant</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\det J</span> measures how much the map stretches area or volume locally. That is why it appears when changing variables in integrals and in probability densities. A linear map <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x</span> has Jacobian <span class=\"m\">A</span> everywhere.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-chain-rule-as-matrix-multiplication\" data-h=\"3\">The chain rule as matrix multiplication</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf G : \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^k</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F : \\mathbb R^k\\to\\mathbb R^m</span>, then the Jacobian of the composition is the product of Jacobians:\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{\\mathbf F\\circ\\mathbf G}(\\mathbf x) = J_{\\mathbf F}(\\mathbf G(\\mathbf x))\\,J_{\\mathbf G}(\\mathbf x).</span>\nThis is the multivariable chain rule written compactly; the matrix product automatically performs the \"multiply along paths, add over paths\" bookkeeping. A deep network is a long chain of such maps, and backprop multiplies (transposed) Jacobians from the output backward.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-hessian-matrix\" data-h=\"3\">The Hessian matrix</h3>\n<p>For a scalar function <span class=\"m\">f : \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R</span>, the Hessian is the <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix of second partials, <span class=\"m\">H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial x_i\\partial x_j}</span>. For two variables:\n<span class=\"mb\">H = \\begin{pmatrix}f_{xx} &amp; f_{xy}\\\\ f_{yx} &amp; f_{yy}\\end{pmatrix}.</span>\nBy Clairaut's theorem <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = f_{yx}</span>, so the Hessian is <strong>symmetric</strong>. For <span class=\"m\">f = x^3 + 2xy + y^2</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_x = 3x^2 + 2y</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = 2x + 2y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{pmatrix}6x &amp; 2\\\\ 2 &amp; 2\\end{pmatrix}</span>. The Hessian is also the Jacobian of the gradient.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Hessian matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">f : \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R</span>, the symmetric <span class=\"m\">n\\times n</span> matrix <span class=\"m\">H</span> with entries <span class=\"m\">H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_i\\,\\partial x_j}</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;saddle&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>The saddle x² − y² has Hessian with entries 2 and −2 on the diagonal: it curves up in one direction and down in the other.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"curvature-and-the-second-order-approximation\" data-h=\"3\">Curvature and the second-order approximation</h3>\n<p>The Hessian gives the quadratic term of the multivariable Taylor expansion:\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) \\approx f(\\mathbf a) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\cdot\\mathbf h + \\tfrac12\\mathbf h^\\top H(\\mathbf a)\\mathbf h.</span>\nThe quantity <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h^\\top H\\mathbf h</span> is the curvature in direction <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h</span>. The eigenvectors of <span class=\"m\">H</span> are the principal directions of curvature and the eigenvalues say how sharply the surface bends along them. If all eigenvalues are positive (<strong>positive definite</strong>), the surface curves up in every direction, like a bowl. If all are negative, it curves down like a dome. Mixed signs mean a saddle. For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 3y^2</span>, <span class=\"m\">H = \\operatorname{diag}(2, 6)</span>: a bowl three times steeper in the <span class=\"m\">y</span> direction than in <span class=\"m\">x</span>. The ratio of the largest to smallest eigenvalue, the <strong>condition number</strong> (here 3), predicts how badly gradient descent will zig-zag.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Positive definite matrix</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A symmetric matrix <span class=\"m\">H</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h^\\top H\\mathbf h &gt; 0</span> for every nonzero <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h</span>; equivalently, all its eigenvalues are positive.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"quadratic-functions-the-model-case\" data-h=\"3\">Quadratic functions: the model case</h3>\n<p>The simplest functions with a Hessian are quadratics, <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf x) = \\frac12\\mathbf x^\\top A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b^\\top\\mathbf x</span> with <span class=\"m\">A</span> symmetric. Their gradient is <span class=\"m\">A\\mathbf x - \\mathbf b</span> and their Hessian is the constant matrix <span class=\"m\">A</span>. Setting the gradient to zero gives the linear system <span class=\"m\">A\\mathbf x = \\mathbf b</span>, so minimizing a quadratic is the same as solving linear equations. Least squares regression is exactly this case, and near any minimum, every smooth loss looks like a quadratic described by its Hessian.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Transposing the Jacobian (rows for inputs instead of outputs) breaks the chain-rule multiplication. Forgetting the factor <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span> in the quadratic term is another frequent slip. And a Hessian with a zero determinant does not tell you the shape on its own; higher-order terms decide.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>The Jacobian collects first derivatives of a vector function, <span class=\"m\">J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial F_i}{\\partial x_j}</span>, and gives the linear approximation <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) \\approx \\mathbf F(\\mathbf a) + J\\mathbf h</span>; the chain rule multiplies Jacobians. The Hessian collects second derivatives of a scalar function, is symmetric, and describes curvature through the quadratic term <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\mathbf h^\\top H\\mathbf h</span>. Its eigenvalues decide bowls, domes and saddles.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-surface\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;mixed&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;slices&quot;, &quot;x0&quot;: 1, &quot;y0&quot;: 0}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The surface x³ − 3x + y². At a critical point the panel uses the Hessian to classify it.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the point at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>, read the classification. Then move <span class=\"m\">x_0</span> to <span class=\"m\">-1</span>.\n2. Rotate the surface to see the two critical points from the side.\n3. Move the point to <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> and look at the pink slice's curvature.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the Hessian is <span class=\"m\">\\begin{pmatrix}6x &amp; 0\\\\ 0 &amp; 2\\end{pmatrix}</span>. At <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> both diagonal entries are positive, so it is a local minimum, a bowl. At <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span> the entries are <span class=\"m\">-6</span> and <span class=\"m\">2</span>, opposite signs, so it is a saddle: the pink slice curves down while the orange slice curves up. At <span class=\"m\">x = 0</span> the pink slice has an inflection, since <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 0</span>.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;saddle&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.05, &quot;sx&quot;: -2, &quot;sy&quot;: 0.05}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Start near the saddle's ridge: plain GD lingers, then escapes along the downhill direction.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Run with plain GD and watch how long the path lingers near the saddle.\n2. Change the starting <span class=\"m\">y</span> to be even closer to 0, and compare with Momentum.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> near a saddle the gradient is tiny, so steps are tiny; only the small negative-curvature component (the Hessian's negative eigenvalue) slowly pushes the point away. This is why saddle points, not local minima, are the main obstacle in high-dimensional training.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial F_i}{\\partial x_j} \\qquad \\mathbf F(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) \\approx \\mathbf F(\\mathbf a) + J(\\mathbf a)\\mathbf h</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{\\mathbf F\\circ\\mathbf G} = J_{\\mathbf F}(\\mathbf G(\\mathbf x))\\,J_{\\mathbf G}(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_i\\partial x_j},\\quad H = H^\\top,\\quad H = J_{\\nabla f}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) \\approx f(\\mathbf a) + \\nabla f^\\top\\mathbf h + \\tfrac12\\mathbf h^\\top H\\mathbf h</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla(\\tfrac12\\mathbf x^\\top A\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x,\\quad H = A \\quad (A \\text{ symmetric})</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobian chain rule</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf G</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf G(\\mathbf x)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F\\circ\\mathbf G</span> is differentiable at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span> with Jacobian <span class=\"m\">J_{\\mathbf F}(\\mathbf G(\\mathbf x))\\,J_{\\mathbf G}(\\mathbf x)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Entry <span class=\"m\">(i, j)</span> of the product is <span class=\"m\">\\sum_k\\frac{\\partial F_i}{\\partial u_k}\\frac{\\partial u_k}{\\partial x_j}</span>, where <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf u = \\mathbf G(\\mathbf x)</span>. By the multivariable chain rule applied to the scalar function <span class=\"m\">F_i(\\mathbf u(\\mathbf x))</span>, this is exactly <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial(F_i\\circ\\mathbf G)}{\\partial x_j}</span>, the <span class=\"m\">(i, j)</span> entry of the composite's Jacobian.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobian of polar coordinates <span class=\"m\">(r, \\theta)\\mapsto(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial r} = \\cos\\theta</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial\\theta} = -r\\sin\\theta</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial r} = \\sin\\theta</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial\\theta} = r\\cos\\theta</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">J = \\begin{pmatrix}\\cos\\theta &amp; -r\\sin\\theta\\\\ \\sin\\theta &amp; r\\cos\\theta\\end{pmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\det J = r\\cos^2\\theta + r\\sin^2\\theta = r</span>: the area factor <span class=\"m\">dA = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Hessian of <span class=\"m\">f = x^2y + y^3</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_x = 2xy</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = x^2 + 3y^2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 2y</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = 2x</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = 6y</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">H(1, 1) = \\begin{pmatrix}2 &amp; 2\\\\ 2 &amp; 6\\end{pmatrix}</span>, determinant <span class=\"m\">12 - 4 = 8 &gt; 0</span> with positive diagonal: positive definite.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Second-order estimate of <span class=\"m\">f = e^x\\cos y</span> at <span class=\"m\">(0.1, 0.2)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>At the origin: <span class=\"m\">f = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (1, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{pmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\\\ 0 &amp; -1\\end{pmatrix}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f \\approx 1 + 0.1 + \\frac12(0.1^2 - 0.2^2) = 1.1 - 0.015</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\approx 1.085</span> (true value <span class=\"m\">1.0831</span>).</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + xy + 2y^2</span>: (a) find the gradient and Hessian; (b) find its eigenvalues and decide the shape; (c) for gradient descent, what is the largest learning rate that still converges?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2x + y,\\ x + 4y)</span>, <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{pmatrix}2 &amp; 1\\\\ 1 &amp; 4\\end{pmatrix}</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">\\det(H - \\lambda I) = (2 - \\lambda)(4 - \\lambda) - 1 = \\lambda^2 - 6\\lambda + 7 = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 3 \\pm\\sqrt2 \\approx 4.41, 1.59</span>. Both positive: a bowl with its minimum at the origin. (c) For a quadratic, GD converges when <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; \\frac{2}{\\lambda_{\\max}} = \\frac{2}{3 + \\sqrt2} \\approx 0.453</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Curvature in training</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The Hessian of the loss determines how fast and how stably gradient descent converges: the step size must satisfy <span class=\"m\">\\eta &lt; \\frac{2}{\\lambda_{\\max}}</span>, and a large condition number <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\lambda_{\\max}}{\\lambda_{\\min}}</span> means slow, zig-zagging progress. Newton's method uses <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w \\leftarrow \\mathbf w - H^{-1}\\nabla L</span>, and practical methods (L-BFGS, natural gradient, Adam's per-coordinate scaling) approximate this cheaply. Normalizing flows compute <span class=\"m\">\\log|\\det J|</span> to transform probability densities.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobians in robotics and graphics</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A robot arm's joint angles determine the hand's position; the Jacobian maps small joint movements to small hand movements, and inverting it (inverse kinematics) tells the controller how to move joints to reach a target. When <span class=\"m\">\\det J = 0</span> the arm is at a singular configuration and loses a direction of motion.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Local stretching on maps</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Map projections distort areas: the absolute determinant of the projection's Jacobian at each point says how much area is magnified there. On the Mercator projection it grows rapidly toward the poles, which is why Greenland looks as big as Africa.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The Jacobian of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F : \\mathbb R^3 \\to \\mathbb R^2</span> has shape...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">3\\times2</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">3\\times3</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Rows are outputs, columns are inputs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + 3y^2</span>, what is <span class=\"m\">H_{22}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = 6</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A Hessian with eigenvalues 4 and <span class=\"m\">-1</span> at a critical point means...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a saddle point</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a local minimum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a local maximum</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Mixed signs.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The determinant of the polar Jacobian at <span class=\"m\">r = 3</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\det J = r</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The Jacobian of the linear map <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">A</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">A^\\top</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>A linear map is its own best linear approximation.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span>, what is the mixed entry <span class=\"m\">H_{12}</span>?</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "multi-optimization": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"finding-the-lowest-point-of-a-landscape\" data-h=\"3\">Finding the lowest point of a landscape</h3>\n<p>In one variable, we found maxima and minima by solving <span class=\"m\">f'(x) = 0</span> and checking the second derivative. With several variables the plan is the same, but the landscape is richer: besides peaks and valleys there are <strong>saddle points</strong>, mountain passes that are a minimum in one direction and a maximum in another. Training a machine learning model is a search for the lowest point of a landscape with millions of dimensions, so understanding critical points in several variables is directly practical.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>A company sells two products at prices <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>. Profit depends on both, partly because the products compete. To find the best pair of prices, it is not enough to optimize each price separately while ignoring the other; you need a point where changing <em>either</em> price, or both together, cannot improve the profit. At such a point the profit surface is flat in every direction: both partial derivatives are zero.</p>\n<h3 id=\"critical-points\" data-h=\"3\">Critical points</h3>\n<p>At a smooth interior maximum or minimum of <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span>, the tangent plane must be horizontal, otherwise you could move uphill or downhill. So\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0, \\text{ i.e. } f_x = 0 \\text{ and } f_y = 0.</span>\nPoints where the gradient is zero (or does not exist) are <strong>critical points</strong>. For <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^2 + y^2 - 4x + 6y</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_x = 2x - 4 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_y = 2y + 6 = 0</span>, so the only critical point is <span class=\"m\">(2, -3)</span>. Completing the square, <span class=\"m\">f = (x - 2)^2 + (y + 3)^2 - 13</span>, confirms it is a minimum with value <span class=\"m\">-13</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Critical point (several variables)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A point <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span> in the domain of <span class=\"m\">f</span> where <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0</span> or where some partial derivative does not exist.</p></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Saddle point</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A critical point that is neither a local maximum nor a local minimum: arbitrarily close to it, <span class=\"m\">f</span> takes values both above and below <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf a)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-second-derivative-test\" data-h=\"3\">The second derivative test</h3>\n<p>To classify a critical point we look at curvature, given by the Hessian <span class=\"m\">H = \\begin{pmatrix}f_{xx} &amp; f_{xy}\\\\ f_{xy} &amp; f_{yy}\\end{pmatrix}</span>. Its determinant <span class=\"m\">D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2</span> decides the shape:</p>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">D &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &gt; 0</span>, the point is a <strong>local minimum</strong> (both eigenvalues positive, a bowl). If <span class=\"m\">D &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &lt; 0</span>, it is a <strong>local maximum</strong> (a dome). If <span class=\"m\">D &lt; 0</span>, it is a <strong>saddle point</strong> (eigenvalues of opposite sign). If <span class=\"m\">D = 0</span>, the test is inconclusive.</p>\n<p>Why the determinant? It is the product of the Hessian's two eigenvalues. A positive product means both have the same sign, so the surface curves the same way in every direction; a negative product means they have opposite signs. Example: <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 - y^2</span> has <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = 0</span>, so <span class=\"m\">D = -4 &lt; 0</span>: a saddle, as its shape shows.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"gradient\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;saddle&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>At a saddle the contours cross. The surface rises in one direction and falls in another, even though the gradient is zero.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-full-example\" data-h=\"3\">A full example</h3>\n<p>Take <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x^3 - 3x + y^2</span>. The gradient is <span class=\"m\">(3x^2 - 3,\\ 2y)</span>, which is zero at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span>. Second partials: <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 6x</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = 0</span>. At <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>: <span class=\"m\">D = 6\\cdot2 = 12 &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 6 &gt; 0</span>, so a local minimum with <span class=\"m\">f = -2</span>. At <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span>: <span class=\"m\">D = -12 &lt; 0</span>, a saddle. Notice the local minimum is not a global one: as <span class=\"m\">x\\to-\\infty</span>, <span class=\"m\">x^3</span> goes to <span class=\"m\">-\\infty</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"global-extrema-on-closed-bounded-regions\" data-h=\"3\">Global extrema on closed, bounded regions</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is continuous on a closed and bounded region (it includes its boundary and does not go off to infinity), it has a global max and a global min, by the multivariable extreme value theorem. To find them, evaluate <span class=\"m\">f</span> at the interior critical points and find the extreme values on the boundary (often by parametrizing it or using Lagrange multipliers, next lesson), then compare. For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + y^2 - 2x</span> on the square <span class=\"m\">[0, 2]\\times[0, 2]</span>: the interior critical point <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> lies on the boundary edge <span class=\"m\">y = 0</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">-1</span>; checking corners gives <span class=\"m\">f(0, 0) = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(2, 0) = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(0, 2) = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(2, 2) = 4</span>. So the min is <span class=\"m\">-1</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> and the max is 4.</p>\n<h3 id=\"least-squares-optimization-you-will-use-constantly\" data-h=\"3\">Least squares: optimization you will use constantly</h3>\n<p>Fit a line <span class=\"m\">y = mx + c</span> to data points <span class=\"m\">(1, 1), (2, 3), (3, 4)</span> by minimizing the sum of squared errors <span class=\"m\">E(m, c) = \\sum(mx_i + c - y_i)^2</span>. Setting both partials to zero gives the <strong>normal equations</strong>: <span class=\"m\">m\\sum x_i^2 + c\\sum x_i = \\sum x_iy_i</span> and <span class=\"m\">m\\sum x_i + cn = \\sum y_i</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">\\sum x = 6</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x^2 = 14</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum y = 8</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum xy = 1 + 6 + 12 = 19</span>, <span class=\"m\">n = 3</span>. So <span class=\"m\">14m + 6c = 19</span> and <span class=\"m\">6m + 3c = 8</span>. Doubling the second gives <span class=\"m\">12m + 6c = 16</span>; subtracting, <span class=\"m\">2m = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">m = 1.5</span>, and <span class=\"m\">c = \\frac{8 - 9}{3} = -\\frac13</span>. Because <span class=\"m\">E</span> is a convex quadratic, this critical point is the global minimum.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Forgetting to solve both equations simultaneously, or missing solutions when factoring, loses critical points. Using only <span class=\"m\">f_{xx}</span> to classify, without <span class=\"m\">D</span>, misses saddles: <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 - 3xy + y^2</span> has <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = f_{yy} = 2 &gt; 0</span> but <span class=\"m\">D = 4 - 9 &lt; 0</span>, a saddle. And a local minimum is not automatically global, unless the function is convex.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Interior extrema occur at critical points, where <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0</span>. Classify them with <span class=\"m\">D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2</span>: <span class=\"m\">D &gt; 0</span> gives a min (<span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &gt; 0</span>) or max (<span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &lt; 0</span>), <span class=\"m\">D &lt; 0</span> a saddle. On closed bounded regions, compare interior critical values with boundary values. Least squares is the classic example, and convex functions guarantee that a critical point is the global minimum.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-surface\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;mixed&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;plane&quot;, &quot;x0&quot;: 1, &quot;y0&quot;: 0}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The surface x³ − 3x + y² with its tangent plane. At the two critical points the plane is horizontal; the panel classifies them.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the point at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>, check that the tangent plane is horizontal and read the classification.\n2. Move the point to <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span> and rotate the view.\n3. Switch to the saddle and the dome and place the point at the origin.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> both <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> and <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span> have horizontal tangent planes, but at <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> the surface lies above the plane (a minimum) while at <span class=\"m\">(-1, 0)</span> it crosses the plane, above in one direction and below in another (a saddle). A zero gradient alone cannot tell them apart; the Hessian test from the article can.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"gd\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;rosen&quot;, &quot;lr&quot;: 0.002}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Gradient descent on the banana-shaped Rosenbrock function: the minimum at (1, 1) sits in a long, curved, flat valley.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Press Run with plain GD and watch how quickly it reaches the valley and how slowly it moves along it.\n2. Switch to Momentum and Adam and compare the paths.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the path drops quickly into the valley (large gradient across it) and then crawls along the nearly flat floor (tiny gradient along it). A badly conditioned Hessian, with very different curvatures in different directions, makes plain gradient descent slow; adaptive methods help.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0 \\text{ at interior extrema}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = \\det H</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D &gt; 0, f_{xx} &gt; 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{min} \\qquad D &gt; 0, f_{xx} &lt; 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{max} \\qquad D &lt; 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{saddle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{least squares: } X^\\top X\\,\\mathbf w = X^\\top\\mathbf y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H \\text{ positive definite everywhere} \\Rightarrow f \\text{ convex} \\Rightarrow \\text{critical point is the global min}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Second derivative test</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">f</span> have continuous second partials near a critical point <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0</span>, and let <span class=\"m\">D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2</span> at <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span>. If <span class=\"m\">D &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &gt; 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span> is a local minimum; if <span class=\"m\">D &gt; 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &lt; 0</span>, a local maximum; if <span class=\"m\">D &lt; 0</span>, a saddle point.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>By the second-order Taylor expansion with <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) - f(\\mathbf a) \\approx \\frac12Q(\\mathbf h)</span> with <span class=\"m\">Q = f_{xx}h_1^2 + 2f_{xy}h_1h_2 + f_{yy}h_2^2</span>. Completing the square (when <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} \\ne 0</span>), <span class=\"m\">Q = f_{xx}\\left(h_1 + \\frac{f_{xy}}{f_{xx}}h_2\\right)^2 + \\frac{D}{f_{xx}}h_2^2</span>. If <span class=\"m\">D &gt; 0</span> both terms have the sign of <span class=\"m\">f_{xx}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">Q</span> has that sign for all <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h \\ne \\mathbf 0</span>. If <span class=\"m\">D &lt; 0</span> the two terms have opposite signs, and choosing <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf h</span> to make either one dominate gives both signs. Continuity of second partials makes the approximation valid near <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Classify the critical points of <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + xy + y^2 - 3y</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_x = 2x + y = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = x + 2y - 3 = 0</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">x = -1</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">D = 4 - 1 = 3 &gt; 0</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &gt; 0</span>: a local (in fact global) minimum, value <span class=\"m\">1 - 2 + 4 - 6 = -3</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Critical points of <span class=\"m\">f = xy(3 - x - y)</span> (interior ones)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f = 3xy - x^2y - xy^2</span>: <span class=\"m\">f_x = y(3 - 2x - y)</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = x(3 - x - 2y)</span>.</li>\n<li>With <span class=\"m\">x, y \\ne 0</span>: <span class=\"m\">2x + y = 3</span> and <span class=\"m\">x + 2y = 3</span> give <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = -2y = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{yy} = -2x = -2</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xy} = 3 - 2x - 2y = -1</span>; <span class=\"m\">D = 4 - 1 = 3 &gt; 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} &lt; 0</span>: a local max with <span class=\"m\">f = 1</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Least squares line through <span class=\"m\">(0, 1), (1, 2), (2, 4)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\sum x = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum x^2 = 5</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum y = 7</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\sum xy = 0 + 2 + 8 = 10</span>, <span class=\"m\">n = 3</span>.</li>\n<li>Normal equations: <span class=\"m\">5m + 3c = 10</span>, <span class=\"m\">3m + 3c = 7</span>. Subtract: <span class=\"m\">2m = 3</span>, <span class=\"m\">m = 1.5</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">c = \\frac{7 - 4.5}{3} = \\frac56</span>: the line <span class=\"m\">y = 1.5x + \\frac56</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A box with no lid must have volume 32 m³. Minimize the surface area <span class=\"m\">S = xy + 2xz + 2yz</span>, where <span class=\"m\">x, y</span> are the base sides and <span class=\"m\">z</span> the height.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>From <span class=\"m\">xyz = 32</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{32}{xy}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">S(x, y) = xy + \\frac{64}{y} + \\frac{64}{x}</span>. <span class=\"m\">S_x = y - \\frac{64}{x^2} = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">S_y = x - \\frac{64}{y^2} = 0</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac{64}{x^2}</span> and substituting, <span class=\"m\">x = \\frac{64x^4}{4096}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x^3 = 64</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 4</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = 2</span>. Check: <span class=\"m\">S_{xx} = \\frac{128}{x^3} = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">S_{yy} = 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">S_{xy} = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">D = 3 &gt; 0</span>: a minimum, <span class=\"m\">S = 16 + 16 + 16 = 48</span> m².</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Saddles and minima in deep learning</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In high dimensions, most critical points of a neural network's loss are saddle points rather than bad local minima: for a point to be a minimum, all of millions of Hessian eigenvalues must be positive. Stochastic gradient noise helps escape saddles. Linear regression is the friendly case: its loss is convex, its normal equations <span class=\"m\">X^\\top X\\mathbf w = X^\\top\\mathbf y</span> give the global minimum directly, and ridge regression adds <span class=\"m\">\\lambda I</span> to <span class=\"m\">X^\\top X</span> to make the Hessian safely positive definite.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Optimization algorithms</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Libraries such as SciPy's <code>minimize</code> implement gradient-based methods (BFGS, L-BFGS, conjugate gradient) that look for points with <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\mathbf 0</span> and use Hessian approximations to take better steps. Checking the Hessian (or its approximation) after convergence tells you whether you found a minimum or got stuck at a saddle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Pricing two products</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A firm's profit from two competing drinks priced at <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> is modelled as <span class=\"m\">P = -2x^2 - 2y^2 + 2xy + 20x + 8y</span>. Setting <span class=\"m\">P_x = -4x + 2y + 20 = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">P_y = -4y + 2x + 8 = 0</span> gives <span class=\"m\">x = 8</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 6</span>, and <span class=\"m\">D = 16 - 4 = 12 &gt; 0</span> with <span class=\"m\">P_{xx} &lt; 0</span>: the profit-maximizing prices.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The critical point of <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + y^2 - 6x + 2y</span> is at <span class=\"m\">x</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">f_x = 2x - 6 = 0</span> (and <span class=\"m\">y = -1</span>).</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">D &lt; 0</span> at a critical point, it is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a saddle point</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a local minimum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a local maximum</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Eigenvalues of opposite sign.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">For <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 - 3xy + y^2</span>, the value of <span class=\"m\">D</span> at the origin is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">2\\cdot2 - (-3)^2</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">D = 5</span> and <span class=\"m\">f_{xx} = -1</span>, the point is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a local maximum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a local minimum</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">a saddle point</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Both curvatures negative.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The minimum value of <span class=\"m\">f = (x - 1)^2 + (y + 2)^2 + 4</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"4\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Attained at <span class=\"m\">(1, -2)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">If <span class=\"m\">D = 0</span>, the second derivative test...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">is inconclusive</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">says it is a saddle</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">says it is a minimum</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Higher-order terms decide.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "lagrange": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"optimizing-with-a-rule-you-must-obey\" data-h=\"3\">Optimizing with a rule you must obey</h3>\n<p>Real optimization problems almost always come with constraints. You want the biggest profit, but your budget is fixed. You want the strongest beam, but its weight is limited. You want the most likely probability distribution, but its probabilities must add to 1. In the previous lesson we searched for the lowest point of a whole landscape. Now we must stay on a particular path through the landscape, the set of points allowed by the constraint, and find the highest or lowest point <em>along that path</em>. The method of <strong>Lagrange multipliers</strong> turns this into a system of equations.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Imagine hiking along a fixed trail that winds across a hillside. You want to know the highest point you will reach on the trail, not the summit of the mountain. Picture the contour lines of the hill crossing your trail. While you climb, the trail cuts across contours, moving you to higher ones. At the highest point of the trail, the trail stops crossing contours and just touches one of them: the trail is <strong>tangent</strong> to a contour line there. If it crossed the contour, you could continue a bit further and go higher.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-key-geometric-idea\" data-h=\"3\">The key geometric idea</h3>\n<p>Write the function to optimize as <span class=\"m\">f(x, y)</span> and the constraint as <span class=\"m\">g(x, y) = c</span>. The constraint is a level curve of <span class=\"m\">g</span>. At a constrained maximum or minimum, the constraint curve is tangent to a level curve of <span class=\"m\">f</span>. Two curves that are tangent at a point have the same perpendicular direction there, and perpendiculars to level curves are gradients. So at the optimum, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g</span> point along the same line:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g, \\qquad g(x, y) = c,</span>\nfor some number <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>, the <strong>Lagrange multiplier</strong>. That gives three equations (<span class=\"m\">f_x = \\lambda g_x</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = \\lambda g_y</span>, <span class=\"m\">g = c</span>) for three unknowns (<span class=\"m\">x, y, \\lambda</span>).</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Lagrange multiplier</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A scalar <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g</span> at a constrained extremum of <span class=\"m\">f</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">g = c</span>. It measures how fast the optimal value changes as the constraint level <span class=\"m\">c</span> changes.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"a-first-example\" data-h=\"3\">A first example</h3>\n<p>Maximize <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) = x + 2y</span> on the unit circle <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = 1</span>. Here <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (1, 2)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g = (2x, 2y)</span>. The equations are <span class=\"m\">1 = 2\\lambda x</span>, <span class=\"m\">2 = 2\\lambda y</span> and <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = 1</span>. From the first two, <span class=\"m\">x = \\frac{1}{2\\lambda}</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac{1}{\\lambda}</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y = 2x</span>. Substituting into the circle, <span class=\"m\">5x^2 = 1</span> and <span class=\"m\">x = \\pm\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}</span>. The two candidates are <span class=\"m\">\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt5}, \\frac{2}{\\sqrt5}\\right)</span> with <span class=\"m\">f = \\frac{5}{\\sqrt5} = \\sqrt5 \\approx 2.236</span> and the opposite point with <span class=\"m\">f = -\\sqrt5</span>. Since a continuous function on a closed, bounded curve has a max and min, these are the maximum and minimum. The answer has a nice interpretation: the maximum is in the direction of <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>, and its value is <span class=\"m\">\\|(1, 2)\\|</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"lagrange\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>Contours of f = x + 2y are straight lines. The largest one that still touches the circle is tangent to it, and there the two gradients are parallel.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-lagrangian\" data-h=\"3\">The Lagrangian</h3>\n<p>It is often convenient to package everything into one function, the <strong>Lagrangian</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathcal L(x, y, \\lambda) = f(x, y) - \\lambda\\big(g(x, y) - c\\big).</span>\nSetting all three partial derivatives of <span class=\"m\">\\mathcal L</span> to zero gives exactly <span class=\"m\">f_x = \\lambda g_x</span>, <span class=\"m\">f_y = \\lambda g_y</span> and <span class=\"m\">g = c</span>. The constrained problem becomes an unconstrained critical point problem in one more variable. With several constraints, add one multiplier per constraint: <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\lambda_1\\nabla g_1 + \\lambda_2\\nabla g_2</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"what-does-lambda-mean\" data-h=\"3\">What does lambda mean?</h3>\n<p>The multiplier is not just an auxiliary variable. If <span class=\"m\">M(c)</span> is the optimal value as a function of the constraint level, then <span class=\"m\">\\frac{dM}{dc} = \\lambda</span>. In economics, if <span class=\"m\">c</span> is a budget, <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> is the extra profit per extra unit of budget, called the <strong>shadow price</strong>. In the circle example with <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = c</span>, the maximum is <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt{5c}</span>, whose derivative at <span class=\"m\">c = 1</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\sqrt5}{2}</span>. And indeed <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\frac{1}{2x} = \\frac{\\sqrt5}{2}</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"a-practical-example\" data-h=\"3\">A practical example</h3>\n<p>A rectangular field next to a river needs fencing on three sides only, with 100 m of fence. Maximize the area <span class=\"m\">A = xy</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + 2y = 100</span> (where <span class=\"m\">x</span> is the side parallel to the river). <span class=\"m\">\\nabla A = (y, x)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g = (1, 2)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y = \\lambda</span> and <span class=\"m\">x = 2\\lambda</span>, giving <span class=\"m\">x = 2y</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">4y = 100</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 25</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 50</span>, and the area is 1250 m². Here <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 25</span>: one more metre of fence would add about 25 m² of area.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Forgetting to include the constraint equation leaves too many unknowns. Dividing by a variable that could be zero loses solutions; consider the zero cases separately. Lagrange gives candidates, so compare <span class=\"m\">f</span> at all of them. Finally, the method needs <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g \\ne \\mathbf 0</span> at the optimum.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>To optimize <span class=\"m\">f</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">g = c</span>, solve <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g</span> together with <span class=\"m\">g = c</span>, then compare the values of <span class=\"m\">f</span> at the solutions. Geometrically, the constraint curve is tangent to a level curve of <span class=\"m\">f</span> at the optimum. The multiplier <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> is the rate at which the optimal value changes with the constraint level.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"lagrange\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Slide along the circle. At the max and min, the pink and green arrows line up.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Slide slowly around the circle and watch the value of <span class=\"m\">f = x + 2y</span> in the panel.\n2. Find the position where <span class=\"m\">f</span> is largest and look at the two arrows.\n3. Find the minimum and compare the arrows' directions there.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> at most positions the gradient of <span class=\"m\">f</span> (fixed, along <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>) and the gradient of the constraint (pointing outward from the circle) point in different directions, and moving along the circle changes <span class=\"m\">f</span>. At the maximum, <span class=\"m\">f \\approx 2.236 = \\sqrt5</span> and the arrows are parallel, the condition <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda &gt; 0</span>. At the minimum they are antiparallel, with <span class=\"m\">\\lambda &lt; 0</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g, \\quad g(\\mathbf x) = c</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\lambda) = f(\\mathbf x) - \\lambda(g(\\mathbf x) - c), \\quad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x, \\lambda}\\mathcal L = \\mathbf 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = \\lambda_1\\nabla g_1 + \\lambda_2\\nabla g_2 \\text{ (two constraints)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dM}{dc} = \\lambda</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\max_{\\|\\mathbf x\\| = 1}\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf x = \\|\\mathbf a\\| \\qquad \\max_{\\|\\mathbf x\\| = 1}\\mathbf x^\\top A\\mathbf x = \\lambda_{\\max}(A)</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Lagrange multiplier theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> and <span class=\"m\">g</span> have continuous first partials, <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf a</span> is a local extremum of <span class=\"m\">f</span> on the set <span class=\"m\">g = c</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g(\\mathbf a) \\ne \\mathbf 0</span>, then there is a number <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a) = \\lambda\\nabla g(\\mathbf a)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r(t)</span> be any smooth curve in the constraint set with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r(0) = \\mathbf a</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">f(\\mathbf r(t))</span> has a local extremum at <span class=\"m\">t = 0</span>, so its derivative <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\cdot\\mathbf r'(0)</span> is 0. So <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a)</span> is perpendicular to every tangent direction of the constraint set. Since <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g(\\mathbf a) \\ne \\mathbf 0</span>, the constraint set is a smooth curve (or surface) whose tangent directions are exactly the vectors perpendicular to <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g(\\mathbf a)</span>. In two dimensions this means <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a)</span> is parallel to <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g(\\mathbf a)</span>, i.e. a multiple of it; the same argument works in <span class=\"m\">n</span> dimensions.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Minimize <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y = 4</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\nabla f = (2x, 2y)</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g = (1, 1)</span>: <span class=\"m\">2x = \\lambda</span>, <span class=\"m\">2y = \\lambda</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x = y</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">2x = 4</span> gives <span class=\"m\">(2, 2)</span>.</li>\n<li>Minimum <span class=\"m\">f = 8</span>: the closest point on the line to the origin, at distance <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt8</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Extremes of <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span> on the ellipse <span class=\"m\">x^2 + 4y^2 = 8</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">y = 2\\lambda x</span> and <span class=\"m\">x = 8\\lambda y</span>. Multiply: <span class=\"m\">xy = 16\\lambda^2xy</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\pm\\frac14</span> (if <span class=\"m\">xy \\ne 0</span>).</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\lambda = \\frac14</span>: <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac x2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x^2 + x^2 = 8</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = \\pm2</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = \\pm1</span> (same signs), <span class=\"m\">f = 2</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\lambda = -\\frac14</span>: opposite signs, <span class=\"m\">f = -2</span>. Max <span class=\"m\">2</span>, min <span class=\"m\">-2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Maximum entropy with two outcomes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Maximize <span class=\"m\">H = -p\\ln p - q\\ln q</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">p + q = 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">-\\ln p - 1 = \\lambda</span> and <span class=\"m\">-\\ln q - 1 = \\lambda</span> give <span class=\"m\">\\ln p = \\ln q</span>, so <span class=\"m\">p = q</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">p = q = \\frac12</span>: the uniform distribution maximizes entropy, with <span class=\"m\">H = \\ln2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A company produces <span class=\"m\">Q = 10x^{0.5}y^{0.5}</span> units using <span class=\"m\">x</span> units of labour at &#36;4 each and <span class=\"m\">y</span> units of capital at &#36;1 each. With a budget of &#36;400, (a) find the production-maximizing <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span>; (b) find the maximum output; (c) interpret <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>Constraint <span class=\"m\">4x + y = 400</span>. (a) <span class=\"m\">Q_x = 5\\sqrt{y/x} = 4\\lambda</span> and <span class=\"m\">Q_y = 5\\sqrt{x/y} = \\lambda</span>. Dividing, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{y}{x} = 4</span>, so <span class=\"m\">y = 4x</span>; then <span class=\"m\">8x = 400</span>, <span class=\"m\">x = 50</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = 200</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">Q = 10\\sqrt{10000} = 1000</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">\\lambda = 5\\sqrt{50/200} = 2.5</span>: one extra dollar of budget raises output by about 2.5 units.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Constraints across machine learning</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Lagrange multipliers are everywhere in ML theory. Support vector machines maximize the margin subject to classification constraints; the multipliers in the dual problem are nonzero only for support vectors. The first principal component maximizes <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w^\\top\\Sigma\\mathbf w</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">\\|\\mathbf w\\| = 1</span>, and the Lagrange condition <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma\\mathbf w = \\lambda\\mathbf w</span> says it is an eigenvector. Maximum-entropy arguments under moment constraints produce softmax and exponential-family distributions. Weight decay can be viewed as a Lagrangian relaxation of a norm constraint.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Constrained solvers</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Optimization software handles constraints with Lagrangian-based methods: augmented Lagrangian, sequential quadratic programming, and interior-point methods for linear programs. The KKT conditions, which extend Lagrange multipliers to inequality constraints, are the stopping test these solvers check.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Spending a budget</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A household splitting a budget between two goods maximizes satisfaction where the ratio of marginal benefits equals the ratio of prices. That is <span class=\"m\">\\nabla U = \\lambda\\nabla(\\text{cost})</span>: the last dollar spent on each good should buy the same extra satisfaction, <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">At a constrained optimum of <span class=\"m\">f</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">g = c</span>, the gradients <span class=\"m\">\\nabla f</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\nabla g</span> are...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">parallel</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">perpendicular</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">both zero</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The constraint curve is tangent to a level curve of <span class=\"m\">f</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The maximum of <span class=\"m\">x + 2y</span> on <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = 1</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\sqrt5</span>. Its square is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The max is <span class=\"m\">\\|(1, 2)\\|</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Minimize <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">x + y = 2</span>. The minimum value is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>At <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">With 100 m of fence on three sides of a riverside field, the maximum area is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1250\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">50\\times25</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The multiplier <span class=\"m\">\\lambda</span> measures...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the rate of change of the optimal value as the constraint level changes</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the size of the gradient of <span class=\"m\">f</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the number of constraints</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac{dM}{dc} = \\lambda</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Maximizing <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf w^\\top\\Sigma\\mathbf w</span> subject to <span class=\"m\">\\|\\mathbf w\\| = 1</span> gives a vector that is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">an eigenvector of <span class=\"m\">\\Sigma</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the zero vector</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">orthogonal to every eigenvector</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\Sigma\\mathbf w = \\lambda\\mathbf w</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "multiple-integrals": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"adding-up-over-a-region\" data-h=\"3\">Adding up over a region</h3>\n<p>In single-variable calculus, the integral <span class=\"m\">\\int_a^b f(x)\\,dx</span> adds up a quantity spread along an interval: the area under a curve, the distance covered from a velocity, the total probability of a range of values. Many quantities are spread over an area or a volume instead. The total rainfall over a country, the mass of a metal plate whose thickness varies, the probability that two random quantities both land in some range: each is a sum over a two- or three-dimensional region. <strong>Double and triple integrals</strong> do this adding up, and, remarkably, they can almost always be computed by doing ordinary one-variable integrals one after another.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>A farmer wants to know how much water is in a rectangular pond whose depth varies from place to place. A sensible approach: lay a grid over the pond, measure the depth in the middle of each grid square, multiply each depth by the square's area to get a small column of water, and add all the columns. Finer grids give better answers. In the limit of infinitely fine grids, the sum becomes the double integral of the depth function over the pond. That is the whole definition; the rest of the lesson is about computing it efficiently.</p>\n<h3 id=\"the-definition-riemann-sums-of-boxes\" data-h=\"3\">The definition: Riemann sums of boxes</h3>\n<p>Let <span class=\"m\">f(x, y) \\ge 0</span> on a rectangle <span class=\"m\">R = [a, b]\\times[c, d]</span>. Split <span class=\"m\">R</span> into small rectangles of area <span class=\"m\">\\Delta A</span>, pick a sample point <span class=\"m\">(x_i, y_j)</span> in each, and form the sum <span class=\"m\">\\sum f(x_i, y_j)\\,\\Delta A</span>. Each term is the volume of a thin box under the surface. As the grid gets finer, the sums approach the <strong>double integral</strong>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_R f(x, y)\\,dA,</span>\nwhich is the volume between the surface <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> and the region <span class=\"m\">R</span>. When <span class=\"m\">f</span> takes negative values, the parts below the plane count negatively, just like signed area in one variable. With <span class=\"m\">f = 1</span>, the double integral is simply the area of <span class=\"m\">R</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Double integral</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\iint_R f\\,dA = \\lim\\sum_{i, j}f(x_i, y_j)\\,\\Delta A</span> as the grid on <span class=\"m\">R</span> is refined: the signed volume under <span class=\"m\">z = f(x, y)</span> over the region <span class=\"m\">R</span>.</p></div></div>\n<p>For a numeric feel, take <span class=\"m\">f = x^2 + y^2</span> on <span class=\"m\">[0, 2]\\times[0, 2]</span> and a <span class=\"m\">4\\times4</span> grid using midpoints. Each square has area <span class=\"m\">0.25</span>, and the midpoints have coordinates <span class=\"m\">0.25, 0.75, 1.25, 1.75</span>. The squares of these add to <span class=\"m\">5.25</span>, so the sum of <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> over all 16 midpoints is <span class=\"m\">2\\times4\\times5.25 = 42</span>, and the box volume is <span class=\"m\">42\\times0.25 = 10.5</span>. The exact answer, computed below, is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{32}{3} \\approx 10.667</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"iterated-integrals-and-fubini-s-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Iterated integrals and Fubini's theorem</h3>\n<p>Adding a grid of boxes can be done row by row. Fix <span class=\"m\">x</span>, add up along <span class=\"m\">y</span>, then add those results along <span class=\"m\">x</span>. In the limit this becomes an <strong>iterated integral</strong>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_R f\\,dA = \\int_a^b\\left(\\int_c^d f(x, y)\\,dy\\right)dx.</span>\nThe inner integral treats <span class=\"m\">x</span> as a constant, exactly like a partial derivative in reverse. For the bowl on <span class=\"m\">[0, 2]^2</span>: the inner integral is <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^2(x^2 + y^2)\\,dy = 2x^2 + \\frac83</span>, and the outer integral is <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^2\\left(2x^2 + \\frac83\\right)dx = \\frac{16}{3} + \\frac{16}{3} = \\frac{32}{3}</span>. <strong>Fubini's theorem</strong> guarantees that for continuous functions the order does not matter: integrating in <span class=\"m\">x</span> first gives the same answer. The inner integral has a nice picture too: it is the area of a cross-section slice of the solid, and the outer integral adds slices, like slicing a loaf of bread.</p>\n<h3 id=\"regions-that-are-not-rectangles\" data-h=\"3\">Regions that are not rectangles</h3>\n<p>Most regions are not rectangles. If a region lies between two curves, <span class=\"m\">g_1(x) \\le y \\le g_2(x)</span> for <span class=\"m\">a \\le x \\le b</span>, the inner limits become functions of <span class=\"m\">x</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_D f\\,dA = \\int_a^b\\int_{g_1(x)}^{g_2(x)}f(x, y)\\,dy\\,dx.</span>\nExample: the region under <span class=\"m\">y = x^2</span> for <span class=\"m\">0 \\le x \\le 1</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\iint_D x\\,dA = \\int_0^1\\int_0^{x^2}x\\,dy\\,dx = \\int_0^1x\\cdot x^2\\,dx = \\frac14</span>. The key skill is sketching the region and reading off the limits: the outer variable's limits are constants, the inner variable's limits may depend on the outer one.</p>\n<p>Sometimes one order is easy and the other is impossible. <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_x^1e^{y^2}\\,dy\\,dx</span> cannot be done as written, since <span class=\"m\">e^{y^2}</span> has no elementary antiderivative. But the region is the triangle <span class=\"m\">0 \\le x \\le y \\le 1</span>, which can also be described as <span class=\"m\">0 \\le y \\le 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le x \\le y</span>. Reversing the order gives <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^ye^{y^2}\\,dx\\,dy = \\int_0^1ye^{y^2}\\,dy = \\frac{e - 1}{2}</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"riemann\" data-args='{}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>One-dimensional Riemann sums add thin rectangles; double integrals add thin boxes over a grid in the plane in exactly the same way.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"average-values-mass-and-probability\" data-h=\"3\">Average values, mass and probability</h3>\n<p>Dividing a double integral by the area of the region gives the <strong>average value</strong> of <span class=\"m\">f</span> there: the average of <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2</span> over <span class=\"m\">[0, 2]^2</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\frac{32/3}{4} = \\frac83</span>. If <span class=\"m\">\\rho(x, y)</span> is a density (mass per unit area), <span class=\"m\">\\iint\\rho\\,dA</span> is the total mass. In probability, a <strong>joint density</strong> <span class=\"m\">p(x, y)</span> satisfies <span class=\"m\">\\iint p\\,dA = 1</span>, and the probability that <span class=\"m\">(X, Y)</span> lands in a region <span class=\"m\">D</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\iint_D p\\,dA</span>. Expected values are <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[g(X, Y)] = \\iint g\\,p\\,dA</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Joint probability density</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) \\ge 0</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\iint_{\\mathbb R^2}p\\,dA = 1</span>, such that <span class=\"m\">P((X, Y)\\in D) = \\iint_Dp(x, y)\\,dA</span> for every region <span class=\"m\">D</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"triple-integrals\" data-h=\"3\">Triple integrals</h3>\n<p>The same ideas work in three dimensions. A <strong>triple integral</strong> <span class=\"m\">\\iiint_E f\\,dV</span> adds <span class=\"m\">f</span> times small volumes <span class=\"m\">\\Delta V</span> over a solid <span class=\"m\">E</span>, and it is computed as three nested one-variable integrals. With <span class=\"m\">f = 1</span> it gives the volume of <span class=\"m\">E</span>; with a density it gives mass. Example: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^1\\int_0^1(x + y + z)\\,dz\\,dy\\,dx</span>. The inner integral gives <span class=\"m\">x + y + \\frac12</span>; the next gives <span class=\"m\">x + 1</span>; the outer gives <span class=\"m\">\\frac32</span>. That matches intuition: the average of <span class=\"m\">x + y + z</span> on the unit cube is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12 + \\frac12 + \\frac12</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>The most common error is putting variables in the outer limits; the outermost limits must always be constants. Another is mismatching the order of <span class=\"m\">dx\\,dy</span> with the order of the limits. When reversing the order, always redraw the region instead of swapping limits mechanically. And remember that <span class=\"m\">dA</span> is an area element: when you change coordinates (next lesson) it picks up a scaling factor.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A double integral adds <span class=\"m\">f\\,\\Delta A</span> over a region and measures signed volume; a triple integral adds <span class=\"m\">f\\,\\Delta V</span> over a solid. By Fubini's theorem both are computed as iterated one-variable integrals, in either order for continuous functions. For non-rectangular regions the inner limits depend on the outer variable. Dividing by area gives averages, and with a joint density the integrals give probabilities and expectations.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-surface\" data-args='{&quot;surface&quot;: &quot;bowl&quot;, &quot;mode&quot;: &quot;boxes&quot;, &quot;n&quot;: 4}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Boxes under the bowl x² + y² over the square [0, 2] × [0, 2]. Increase n and watch the total volume approach the exact integral.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With <span class=\"m\">n = 4</span>, read the sum of box volumes in the panel and compare it with the article's 10.5.\n2. Increase <span class=\"m\">n</span> step by step up to 16 and record how the sum changes.\n3. Rotate the view to see the boxes from the side, then switch to the dome and to the saddle.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the sums approach <span class=\"m\">\\frac{32}{3} \\approx 10.667</span>, the exact value from the iterated integral; the midpoint boxes slightly underestimate because the bowl curves upward. For the saddle the sum can be zero or negative, since boxes below the plane count negatively, the 2D version of signed area.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\iint_R f\\,dA = \\lim\\sum f(x_i, y_j)\\,\\Delta A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_{[a,b]\\times[c,d]}f\\,dA = \\int_a^b\\int_c^d f\\,dy\\,dx = \\int_c^d\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\,dy</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_D f\\,dA = \\int_a^b\\int_{g_1(x)}^{g_2(x)}f\\,dy\\,dx</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{area}(D) = \\iint_D1\\,dA \\qquad \\bar f = \\frac{1}{\\text{area}(D)}\\iint_D f\\,dA</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_{[a,b]\\times[c,d]}g(x)h(y)\\,dA = \\left(\\int_a^b g\\right)\\left(\\int_c^d h\\right)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P((X, Y)\\in D) = \\iint_D p\\,dA \\qquad \\mathbb E[g(X, Y)] = \\iint g\\,p\\,dA</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Fubini's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">f</span> is continuous on the rectangle <span class=\"m\">R = [a, b]\\times[c, d]</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\iint_R f\\,dA = \\int_a^b\\int_c^d f(x, y)\\,dy\\,dx = \\int_c^d\\int_a^b f(x, y)\\,dx\\,dy</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(Idea.) Group the Riemann sum <span class=\"m\">\\sum_{i, j}f(x_i, y_j)\\Delta x\\Delta y</span> by columns: <span class=\"m\">\\sum_i\\left(\\sum_jf(x_i, y_j)\\Delta y\\right)\\Delta x</span>. The inner sum is a Riemann sum for <span class=\"m\">\\int_c^d f(x_i, y)\\,dy</span>, and the outer sum is then a Riemann sum for the integral of that function of <span class=\"m\">x</span>. Continuity on the closed rectangle (hence uniform continuity) makes both limits behave, so the double limit equals the iterated integral. Grouping by rows instead gives the other order.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Evaluate <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^2(x + 2y)\\,dy\\,dx</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Inner (treat <span class=\"m\">x</span> as a constant): <span class=\"m\">\\left[xy + y^2\\right]_0^2 = 2x + 4</span>.</li>\n<li>Outer: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1(2x + 4)\\,dx = \\left[x^2 + 4x\\right]_0^1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">= 5</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Integrate <span class=\"m\">f = xy</span> over the triangle with vertices <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span>, <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>The region is <span class=\"m\">0 \\le x \\le 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le y \\le x</span>.</li>\n<li>Inner: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^x xy\\,dy = \\frac{x^3}{2}</span>.</li>\n<li>Outer: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\frac{x^3}{2}\\,dx = \\frac18</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Reverse the order in <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_x^1e^{y^2}\\,dy\\,dx</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Region: <span class=\"m\">0 \\le x \\le y \\le 1</span>, the same as <span class=\"m\">0 \\le y \\le 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le x \\le y</span>.</li>\n<li>New integral: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^ye^{y^2}\\,dx\\,dy = \\int_0^1ye^{y^2}\\,dy</span>.</li>\n<li>Substitution <span class=\"m\">u = y^2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(e - 1) \\approx 0.859</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>The pair <span class=\"m\">(X, Y)</span> has joint density <span class=\"m\">p(x, y) = c(x + y)</span> on the unit square <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]^2</span> (and 0 elsewhere). (a) Find <span class=\"m\">c</span>. (b) Find <span class=\"m\">P\\left(X \\le \\frac12, Y \\le \\frac12\\right)</span>. (c) Find <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[X]</span>.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\iint(x + y)\\,dA = \\frac12 + \\frac12 = 1</span>, so <span class=\"m\">c = 1</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{1/2}\\int_0^{1/2}(x + y)\\,dy\\,dx = \\int_0^{1/2}\\left(\\frac x2 + \\frac18\\right)dx = \\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{1}{16} = \\frac18</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[X] = \\int_0^1\\int_0^1x(x + y)\\,dy\\,dx = \\int_0^1\\left(x^2 + \\frac x2\\right)dx = \\frac13 + \\frac14 = \\frac{7}{12}</span>.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Expectations over several variables</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Every expected loss in ML is a multiple integral: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb E[L] = \\iint L(\\mathbf x, y)\\,p(\\mathbf x, y)\\,d\\mathbf x\\,dy</span> over the data distribution. Marginalizing a joint density, <span class=\"m\">p(x) = \\int p(x, y)\\,dy</span>, is a single slice of Fubini's theorem, and it is how latent-variable models such as VAEs define the likelihood <span class=\"m\">p(\\mathbf x) = \\int p(\\mathbf x\\mid\\mathbf z)p(\\mathbf z)\\,d\\mathbf z</span>. Because these integrals are high-dimensional, ML estimates them by Monte Carlo sampling instead of grids.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Monte Carlo integration and the curse of dimensionality</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A grid with 100 points per axis needs <span class=\"m\">100^d</span> evaluations in <span class=\"m\">d</span> dimensions, impossible beyond a handful of dimensions. Monte Carlo integration averages <span class=\"m\">f</span> at random points; its error shrinks like <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt N}</span> regardless of dimension. Graphics renderers compute the light reaching each pixel this way, as an integral over directions.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Rainfall over a region</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Weather services estimate total rainfall over a catchment by integrating radar-measured rain depth over the area: depth (in metres) times area (in square metres) summed over grid cells gives cubic metres of water flowing to a river, the double integral <span class=\"m\">\\iint d(x, y)\\,dA</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\int_0^2\\int_0^3 1\\,dy\\,dx</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The area of a <span class=\"m\">2\\times3</span> rectangle.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^1xy\\,dy\\,dx</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/4|0.25\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\cdot\\frac12</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The average value of <span class=\"m\">f = x + y</span> over <span class=\"m\">[0, 2]\\times[0, 2]</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>By symmetry, <span class=\"m\">1 + 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The area of the triangle <span class=\"m\">0 \\le y \\le x \\le 1</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1\\int_0^x dy\\,dx</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\int_0^1x\\,dx</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Fubini's theorem says that for continuous <span class=\"m\">f</span> on a rectangle...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">the order of integration does not change the result</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the double integral is always positive</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">you must integrate in <span class=\"m\">x</span> first</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Both iterated integrals equal the double integral.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In an iterated integral, the outermost limits must be...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">constants</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">functions of the inner variable</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">zero</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Only inner limits may depend on outer variables.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "change-of-variables": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"choosing-the-right-coordinates\" data-h=\"3\">Choosing the right coordinates</h3>\n<p>Some integrals are painful in <span class=\"m\">x</span> and <span class=\"m\">y</span> but easy in other coordinates. Integrating over a disc in Cartesian coordinates forces square roots into the limits, <span class=\"m\">-\\sqrt{4 - x^2} \\le y \\le \\sqrt{4 - x^2}</span>, while in polar coordinates the same disc is simply <span class=\"m\">0 \\le r \\le 2</span>, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi</span>. Changing variables is the multivariable version of u-substitution. There is one catch: when you change coordinates, the little area elements change size, and you must account for that with a scaling factor, the <strong>Jacobian determinant</strong>. Get the factor right and you can compute volumes of spheres, the famous Gaussian integral behind the normal distribution, and the densities of transformed random variables.</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Think of a pizza. Cutting it into a square grid is awkward: the pieces near the crust are oddly shaped. Cutting it into slices and rings is natural. But notice that a piece of the outer ring is bigger than a piece of an inner ring with the same angle and the same ring width, because it is farther from the centre. A piece at radius <span class=\"m\">r</span> with angular width <span class=\"m\">\\Delta\\theta</span> and radial width <span class=\"m\">\\Delta r</span> is approximately a rectangle of sides <span class=\"m\">r\\,\\Delta\\theta</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\Delta r</span>, so its area is about <span class=\"m\">r\\,\\Delta r\\,\\Delta\\theta</span>. That extra factor <span class=\"m\">r</span> is the whole story of polar integration.</p>\n<h3 id=\"polar-coordinates\" data-h=\"3\">Polar coordinates</h3>\n<p>A point is described by its distance <span class=\"m\">r</span> from the origin and its angle <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span>, with <span class=\"m\">x = r\\cos\\theta</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = r\\sin\\theta</span>, so <span class=\"m\">x^2 + y^2 = r^2</span>. As the pizza argument shows, the area element becomes\n<span class=\"mb\">dA = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.</span>\nSo <span class=\"m\">\\iint_D f(x, y)\\,dA = \\iint f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span>. Check with the area of a disc of radius <span class=\"m\">R</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^R r\\,dr\\,d\\theta = 2\\pi\\cdot\\frac{R^2}{2} = \\pi R^2</span>. Forgetting the <span class=\"m\">r</span> would give <span class=\"m\">2\\pi R</span>, which is not even an area.</p>\n<p>Example: <span class=\"m\">\\iint_D(x^2 + y^2)\\,dA</span> over the disc of radius 2. In polar coordinates the integrand is <span class=\"m\">r^2</span>, so the integral is <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^2r^2\\cdot r\\,dr\\,d\\theta = 2\\pi\\cdot\\frac{2^4}{4} = 8\\pi</span>.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Polar area element</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>In polar coordinates <span class=\"m\">x = r\\cos\\theta</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = r\\sin\\theta</span>, the area element is <span class=\"m\">dA = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-general-rule-the-jacobian-determinant\" data-h=\"3\">The general rule: the Jacobian determinant</h3>\n<p>Where did <span class=\"m\">r</span> come from algebraically? Any change of variables <span class=\"m\">(u, v)\\mapsto(x(u, v), y(u, v))</span> maps a tiny rectangle of sides <span class=\"m\">\\Delta u</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\Delta v</span> to a tiny (approximately) parallelogram. Near a point, the map acts like its Jacobian matrix <span class=\"m\">J = \\frac{\\partial(x, y)}{\\partial(u, v)}</span>, and a linear map scales areas by the absolute value of its determinant. So\n<span class=\"mb\">dA = \\left|\\det\\frac{\\partial(x, y)}{\\partial(u, v)}\\right|du\\,dv,</span>\nand\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_D f(x, y)\\,dx\\,dy = \\iint_{D'}f(x(u, v), y(u, v))\\left|\\det J\\right|du\\,dv.</span>\nFor polar coordinates, <span class=\"m\">\\det J = \\cos\\theta\\cdot r\\cos\\theta - (-r\\sin\\theta)\\sin\\theta = r</span>, matching the pizza argument.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Jacobian determinant</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For a change of variables <span class=\"m\">(u, v)\\mapsto(x, y)</span>, the determinant <span class=\"m\">\\det\\frac{\\partial(x, y)}{\\partial(u, v)} = x_uy_v - x_vy_u</span>. Its absolute value is the local area-scaling factor.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"linear-changes-of-variables\" data-h=\"3\">Linear changes of variables</h3>\n<p>For a linear map the scaling is the same everywhere. Consider a region bounded by the lines <span class=\"m\">x + y = 0</span>, <span class=\"m\">x + y = 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">x - y = 0</span> and <span class=\"m\">x - y = 2</span>, a tilted rectangle. Let <span class=\"m\">u = x + y</span> and <span class=\"m\">v = x - y</span>, so the region becomes the plain rectangle <span class=\"m\">0 \\le u \\le 1</span>, <span class=\"m\">0 \\le v \\le 2</span>. Solving, <span class=\"m\">x = \\frac{u + v}{2}</span> and <span class=\"m\">y = \\frac{u - v}{2}</span>, and the Jacobian determinant is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\cdot\\left(-\\frac12\\right) - \\frac12\\cdot\\frac12 = -\\frac12</span>. So <span class=\"m\">dA = \\frac12\\,du\\,dv</span>. The area of the region is <span class=\"m\">\\frac12\\times1\\times2 = 1</span>, and <span class=\"m\">\\iint(x + y)\\,dA = \\int_0^1\\int_0^2u\\cdot\\frac12\\,dv\\,du = \\frac12</span>.</p>\n<figure class=\"viz fig\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;symmetric&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption>A linear map turns the unit square into a parallelogram; the determinant (here 3) is the factor by which every area is multiplied.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3 id=\"the-gaussian-integral\" data-h=\"3\">The Gaussian integral</h3>\n<p>The most famous use of polar coordinates computes <span class=\"m\">I = \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}\\,dx</span>, which has no elementary antiderivative. Square it and write the product as a double integral: <span class=\"m\">I^2 = \\int e^{-x^2}dx\\int e^{-y^2}dy = \\iint_{\\mathbb R^2}e^{-(x^2 + y^2)}\\,dA</span>. In polar coordinates this is <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\infty e^{-r^2}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span>. The factor <span class=\"m\">r</span> makes the inner integral easy with <span class=\"m\">u = r^2</span>: it equals <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>. So <span class=\"m\">I^2 = 2\\pi\\cdot\\frac12 = \\pi</span> and <span class=\"m\">I = \\sqrt\\pi</span>. This is exactly why the normal density has the constant <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}}</span> in front.</p>\n<h3 id=\"cylindrical-and-spherical-coordinates\" data-h=\"3\">Cylindrical and spherical coordinates</h3>\n<p>In three dimensions, <strong>cylindrical coordinates</strong> use polar coordinates in the <span class=\"m\">xy</span>-plane plus the height <span class=\"m\">z</span>, with <span class=\"m\">dV = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\,dz</span>. They suit cylinders and cones. <strong>Spherical coordinates</strong> use the distance <span class=\"m\">\\rho</span> from the origin, the angle <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> down from the positive <span class=\"m\">z</span>-axis, and the angle <span class=\"m\">\\theta</span> around it: <span class=\"m\">x = \\rho\\sin\\varphi\\cos\\theta</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = \\rho\\sin\\varphi\\sin\\theta</span>, <span class=\"m\">z = \\rho\\cos\\varphi</span>, with\n<span class=\"mb\">dV = \\rho^2\\sin\\varphi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta.</span>\nThe volume of a ball of radius <span class=\"m\">R</span> is then <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^R\\rho^2\\sin\\varphi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta = 2\\pi\\cdot2\\cdot\\frac{R^3}{3} = \\frac43\\pi R^3</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"transforming-probability-densities\" data-h=\"3\">Transforming probability densities</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf Y = T(\\mathbf X)</span> is an invertible smooth transformation of a random vector, probability must be conserved: mass in a small region of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x</span>-space ends up in the image region, whose area is scaled by <span class=\"m\">|\\det J_T|</span>. So <span class=\"m\">p_Y(\\mathbf y) = p_X(\\mathbf x)\\,\\left|\\det J_T(\\mathbf x)\\right|^{-1}</span> with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf x = T^{-1}(\\mathbf y)</span>. In one dimension: if <span class=\"m\">X</span> is uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y = 2X</span>, then <span class=\"m\">Y</span> is spread over <span class=\"m\">[0, 2]</span> with density <span class=\"m\">\\frac12</span>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Forgetting the Jacobian factor (<span class=\"m\">r</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\rho^2\\sin\\varphi</span>) is by far the most common error. Others: using the Jacobian of the wrong direction (you need <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial(x, y)}{\\partial(u, v)}</span>, old variables in terms of new), forgetting the absolute value, and not transforming the region's limits along with the integrand.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>Changing variables lets you integrate in coordinates that fit the region. Every change of variables multiplies the area or volume element by <span class=\"m\">|\\det J|</span>: <span class=\"m\">r</span> for polar, <span class=\"m\">r</span> for cylindrical, <span class=\"m\">\\rho^2\\sin\\varphi</span> for spherical, a constant for linear maps. Polar coordinates give <span class=\"m\">\\int e^{-x^2}dx = \\sqrt\\pi</span>, and the same determinant rule transforms probability densities.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"matrix2\" data-args='{&quot;preset&quot;: &quot;symmetric&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> A linear map acting on the plane. The determinant shown in the panel is the factor by which it scales every area.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the symmetric preset, read <span class=\"m\">\\det A</span> and compare the area of the transformed square with the original unit square.\n2. Drag <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\imath</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\hat\\jmath</span> to make the determinant larger, then smaller, then 0.\n3. Choose the rotate and shear presets and read their determinants.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the area of the image of the unit square is always <span class=\"m\">|\\det A|</span>, which is why the Jacobian determinant appears in <span class=\"m\">dA = |\\det J|\\,du\\,dv</span>. Rotations and shears have determinant 1, so they preserve area. When the determinant is 0, the plane is squashed onto a line and the change of variables cannot be undone.</p></div></div>\n<figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"distribution\" data-args='{&quot;kind&quot;: &quot;normal&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> The normal density. Its total area is 1 because of the polar-coordinates computation of the Gaussian integral.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. Look at the curve's peak height for the standard normal and compare it with <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\pi}} \\approx 0.399</span>.\n2. Change the spread parameter and watch the peak height change.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> making the curve twice as wide halves its height, so the area stays 1. That is the one-dimensional change of variables <span class=\"m\">Y = \\sigma X</span>: the density is divided by the stretching factor <span class=\"m\">|\\det J| = \\sigma</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\iint_D f\\,dx\\,dy = \\iint_{D'}f(x(u, v), y(u, v))\\left|\\det\\frac{\\partial(x, y)}{\\partial(u, v)}\\right|du\\,dv</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{polar: } dA = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\qquad \\text{cylindrical: } dV = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\,dz</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{spherical: } dV = \\rho^2\\sin\\varphi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}dx = \\sqrt\\pi \\qquad \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2/2}dx = \\sqrt{2\\pi}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p_Y(\\mathbf y) = p_X(T^{-1}(\\mathbf y))\\left|\\det J_{T^{-1}}(\\mathbf y)\\right|</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Change of variables theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">T(u, v) = (x(u, v), y(u, v))</span> be a one-to-one map with continuous first partials from a region <span class=\"m\">D'</span> onto <span class=\"m\">D</span>, with nonzero Jacobian determinant. Then for continuous <span class=\"m\">f</span>, <span class=\"m\">\\iint_D f(x, y)\\,dA = \\iint_{D'}f(T(u, v))\\,\\left|\\det J_T(u, v)\\right|\\,du\\,dv</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(Idea.) Divide <span class=\"m\">D'</span> into tiny rectangles of area <span class=\"m\">\\Delta u\\Delta v</span>. By the linear approximation, <span class=\"m\">T</span> maps each one to approximately a parallelogram spanned by <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf T_u\\Delta u</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf T_v\\Delta v</span>, whose area is <span class=\"m\">|\\det J_T|\\,\\Delta u\\Delta v</span>. So the Riemann sum <span class=\"m\">\\sum f(x, y)\\,\\Delta A</span> over <span class=\"m\">D</span> matches <span class=\"m\">\\sum f(T(u, v))\\,|\\det J_T|\\,\\Delta u\\Delta v</span> over <span class=\"m\">D'</span> up to errors that vanish as the grid is refined.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Compute <span class=\"m\">\\iint_D e^{-(x^2 + y^2)}\\,dA</span> over the disc of radius 1</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Polar: <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^1e^{-r^2}r\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span>.</li>\n<li>Inner, with <span class=\"m\">u = r^2</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\frac12(1 - e^{-1})</span>.</li>\n<li>Total: <span class=\"m\">\\pi(1 - e^{-1}) \\approx 1.986</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Area of the ellipse <span class=\"m\">\\frac{x^2}{a^2} + \\frac{y^2}{b^2} \\le 1</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Let <span class=\"m\">x = au</span>, <span class=\"m\">y = bv</span>; the region becomes the unit disc <span class=\"m\">u^2 + v^2 \\le 1</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\det J = ab</span>, so <span class=\"m\">dA = ab\\,du\\,dv</span>.</li>\n<li>Area <span class=\"m\">= ab\\times\\pi = \\pi ab</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Volume of a cone of height <span class=\"m\">h</span> and base radius <span class=\"m\">R</span>, using cylindrical coordinates</div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>At radius <span class=\"m\">r</span>, the cone's height runs from <span class=\"m\">z = \\frac{h}{R}r</span> up to <span class=\"m\">h</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">V = \\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^R\\left(h - \\frac hRr\\right)r\\,dr\\,d\\theta = 2\\pi\\left(\\frac{hR^2}{2} - \\frac{hR^2}{3}\\right)</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">V = \\frac13\\pi R^2h</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>(a) Use polar coordinates to find the area of the annulus <span class=\"m\">1 \\le x^2 + y^2 \\le 9</span>. (b) Evaluate <span class=\"m\">\\iint_D\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2}}\\,dA</span> over the same annulus. (c) A random point <span class=\"m\">(X, Y)</span> is uniform on the disc of radius 1. Find the density of its distance <span class=\"m\">R</span> from the centre.</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^3r\\,dr\\,d\\theta = 2\\pi\\cdot\\frac{9 - 1}{2} = 8\\pi</span>. (b) <span class=\"m\">\\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_1^3\\frac1r\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta = 2\\pi\\cdot2 = 4\\pi</span>. (c) The joint density is <span class=\"m\">\\frac1\\pi</span> on the disc. <span class=\"m\">P(R \\le s) = \\frac{\\pi s^2}{\\pi} = s^2</span> for <span class=\"m\">0 \\le s \\le 1</span>, so the density is <span class=\"m\">2s</span>: points are more likely far from the centre, because outer rings have more area (the factor <span class=\"m\">r</span> again).</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Normalizing flows and the reparameterization</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Normalizing flows build complex distributions by pushing a simple Gaussian through invertible neural networks and track the density with the change-of-variables formula: <span class=\"m\">\\log p_Y(\\mathbf y) = \\log p_X(\\mathbf x) - \\log|\\det J_T(\\mathbf x)|</span>. Architectures like RealNVP are designed so that this determinant is cheap (triangular Jacobians). The normalizing constant <span class=\"m\">\\frac{1}{(2\\pi)^{d/2}|\\Sigma|^{1/2}}</span> of the multivariate Gaussian comes from the Gaussian integral plus a linear change of variables.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Sampling random points</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>To sample a uniform random point in a disc, generating <span class=\"m\">r</span> uniformly in <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]</span> is wrong: points cluster at the centre. The change-of-variables density <span class=\"m\">2r</span> shows the correct method, <span class=\"m\">r = \\sqrt U</span> with <span class=\"m\">U</span> uniform. The Box–Muller method generates normal random numbers from two uniforms using polar coordinates and the Gaussian integral.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Planets and pipes</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Engineers compute the mass of a pipe or the flow through a circular cross-section in cylindrical coordinates, and astronomers compute the mass of a planet with density depending on depth, <span class=\"m\">M = \\int_0^{2\\pi}\\int_0^\\pi\\int_0^R\\rho(s)s^2\\sin\\varphi\\,ds\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta = 4\\pi\\int_0^R\\rho(s)s^2\\,ds</span>, in spherical coordinates.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">In polar coordinates, the area element at radius <span class=\"m\">r = 3</span> is <span class=\"m\">dA = c\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span> with <span class=\"m\">c</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"3\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">dA = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The map <span class=\"m\">(u, v)\\mapsto(2u, 3v)</span> multiplies areas by...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\det\\begin{pmatrix}2 &amp; 0\\\\ 0 &amp; 3\\end{pmatrix}</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The area of the ellipse <span class=\"m\">\\frac{x^2}{4} + \\frac{y^2}{9} \\le 1</span> is <span class=\"m\">k\\pi</span> with <span class=\"m\">k</span> =</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\pi ab = \\pi\\cdot2\\cdot3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">X</span> is uniform on <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]</span> and <span class=\"m\">Y = 2X</span>. The density of <span class=\"m\">Y</span> on <span class=\"m\">[0, 2]</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"1/2|0.5\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Stretching by 2 halves the density.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The spherical volume element is...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\rho^2\\sin\\varphi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\rho\\,d\\rho\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">\\sin\\varphi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>The Jacobian determinant of spherical coordinates.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\"><span class=\"m\">\\left(\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}dx\\right)^2</span> equals...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\"><span class=\"m\">\\pi</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">2\\pi</span></button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\"><span class=\"m\">1</span></button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Polar coordinates give <span class=\"m\">2\\pi\\cdot\\frac12</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>", "vector-fields": "<section class=\"lsec lsec-learn\" id=\"learn\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">1</span><div><h2>Learn</h2><p>Read this first. The idea explained from the ground up.</p></div></div>\n<h3 id=\"arrows-everywhere\" data-h=\"3\">Arrows everywhere</h3>\n<p>So far our functions have produced a single number at each point. Many things in nature attach an <strong>arrow</strong> to each point instead: the velocity of water in a river, the wind at every place on a weather map, the force of gravity around a planet, the electric field around a charge. A <strong>vector field</strong> assigns a vector to every point of the plane or space. We have already met one: the gradient of a function is a vector field, with arrows pointing uphill. This lesson introduces the tools for understanding fields: how much they spread out (divergence), how much they swirl (curl), and how much work they do along a path (line integrals).</p>\n<h3 id=\"an-everyday-scenario\" data-h=\"3\">An everyday scenario</h3>\n<p>Drop a few leaves into a stream and watch them. Some regions push the leaves along steadily. Near a spring bubbling up from the bottom, leaves move outward in all directions: the flow is <strong>diverging</strong>. Near an eddy behind a rock, leaves circle round: the flow has <strong>curl</strong>. And if you paddle a canoe from one bank to the other, the current helps on some stretches and hinders on others; the total help is a line integral. These three ideas describe nearly everything about a flow.</p>\n<h3 id=\"vector-fields\" data-h=\"3\">Vector fields</h3>\n<p>A vector field in the plane is a function <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(x, y) = (P(x, y), Q(x, y))</span>. Picture it by drawing the arrow <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(x, y)</span> at a grid of points. Three basic examples: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (x, y)</span> points straight away from the origin, a <strong>source</strong>; <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (-x, -y)</span> points inward, a <strong>sink</strong>; and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (-y, x)</span> circles counterclockwise around the origin, a <strong>rotation</strong>. At the point <span class=\"m\">(1, 0)</span> the rotation field is <span class=\"m\">(0, 1)</span>, pointing straight up, tangent to the circle through that point. A particle carried by a field follows a <strong>flow line</strong>, a curve whose velocity at every point equals the field's arrow there; for the rotation field the flow lines are circles.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Vector field</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A function <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F : \\mathbb R^n\\to\\mathbb R^n</span> assigning a vector to every point, written in 2D as <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (P, Q)</span>.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"gradient-fields-and-potentials\" data-h=\"3\">Gradient fields and potentials</h3>\n<p>If <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\varphi</span> for some scalar function <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span>, the field is called a <strong>gradient field</strong> or <strong>conservative</strong>, and <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> is a <strong>potential</strong>. For example <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (y, x)</span> is the gradient of <span class=\"m\">\\varphi = xy</span>. Gravity near the Earth is the (negative) gradient of potential energy. Not every field is a gradient: the rotation field <span class=\"m\">(-y, x)</span> is not. A quick test in 2D uses Clairaut's theorem: if <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (P, Q) = (\\varphi_x, \\varphi_y)</span>, then <span class=\"m\">P_y = \\varphi_{xy} = \\varphi_{yx} = Q_x</span>. So a gradient field must satisfy <span class=\"m\">\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}</span>. For <span class=\"m\">(-y, x)</span>, <span class=\"m\">Q_x = 1</span> but <span class=\"m\">P_y = -1</span>, so it fails.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Conservative field</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>A vector field <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F</span> that equals <span class=\"m\">\\nabla\\varphi</span> for some scalar potential <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span>. Its line integrals depend only on the endpoints of the path.</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"line-integrals-and-work\" data-h=\"3\">Line integrals and work</h3>\n<p>When a force <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F</span> pushes an object along a curve <span class=\"m\">C</span>, only the component of force along the direction of motion does work. Splitting the path into tiny steps <span class=\"m\">d\\mathbf r</span>, the work is the sum of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r</span>:\n<span class=\"mb\">W = \\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt,</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r(t)</span>, <span class=\"m\">a \\le t \\le b</span>, traces the curve. Example: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (y, x)</span> along the parabola <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r(t) = (t, t^2)</span> from <span class=\"m\">t = 0</span> to <span class=\"m\">1</span>. Then <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r) = (t^2, t)</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r' = (1, 2t)</span>, so the integrand is <span class=\"m\">t^2 + 2t^2 = 3t^2</span> and the work is 1.</p>\n<p>For gradient fields there is a shortcut, the <strong>fundamental theorem for line integrals</strong>: <span class=\"m\">\\int_C\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot d\\mathbf r = \\varphi(\\text{end}) - \\varphi(\\text{start})</span>. Since <span class=\"m\">(y, x) = \\nabla(xy)</span>, the work above is <span class=\"m\">xy</span> at <span class=\"m\">(1, 1)</span> minus <span class=\"m\">xy</span> at <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span>, which is 1, with no integral needed. The path does not matter, and around any closed loop the work is zero. That is what \"conservative\" means: energy is conserved.</p>\n<h3 id=\"divergence-spreading-out\" data-h=\"3\">Divergence: spreading out</h3>\n<p>The <strong>divergence</strong> of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (P, Q)</span> is <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F = \\nabla\\cdot\\mathbf F = P_x + Q_y</span>. It measures the net outflow per unit area from a tiny region around a point. The source <span class=\"m\">(x, y)</span> has divergence <span class=\"m\">1 + 1 = 2</span> everywhere: fluid is being created and flows out. The sink has divergence <span class=\"m\">-2</span>. The rotation field has divergence <span class=\"m\">0 + 0 = 0</span>: fluid just circulates, neither created nor destroyed. Fields with zero divergence describe incompressible flow.</p>\n<h3 id=\"curl-swirling\" data-h=\"3\">Curl: swirling</h3>\n<p>The (scalar) <strong>curl</strong> of a 2D field is <span class=\"m\">Q_x - P_y</span>. It measures the circulation per unit area around a point, how strongly a tiny paddle wheel placed there would spin (counterclockwise positive). For the rotation <span class=\"m\">(-y, x)</span>, the curl is <span class=\"m\">1 - (-1) = 2</span>. For the source it is 0. For the shear flow <span class=\"m\">(y, 0)</span>, like a river faster at one bank, the curl is <span class=\"m\">0 - 1 = -1</span>: even though the arrows are all parallel, a paddle wheel spins because one side is pushed harder than the other. Gradient fields always have zero curl, by the Clairaut argument above. In 3D the curl is a vector, <span class=\"m\">\\nabla\\times\\mathbf F</span>, whose direction is the spin axis.</p>\n<div class=\"box def\"><div class=\"box-label\">Definition</div><div class=\"box-title\">Divergence and curl (2D)</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>For <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (P, Q)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F = P_x + Q_y</span> (outflow per unit area) and <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F = Q_x - P_y</span> (circulation per unit area).</p></div></div>\n\n<h3 id=\"green-s-theorem\" data-h=\"3\">Green's theorem</h3>\n<p>Adding up curl over a region gives the circulation around its boundary. <strong>Green's theorem</strong> says\n<span class=\"mb\">\\oint_C P\\,dx + Q\\,dy = \\iint_D(Q_x - P_y)\\,dA,</span>\nwhere <span class=\"m\">C</span> is the counterclockwise boundary of <span class=\"m\">D</span>. For the rotation field around a circle of radius <span class=\"m\">r</span>, the curl is 2 everywhere, so the circulation is <span class=\"m\">2\\times\\pi r^2 = 2\\pi r^2</span>. Checking directly with <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r = (r\\cos t, r\\sin t)</span>: <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf r' = r^2\\sin^2t + r^2\\cos^2t = r^2</span>, integrated over <span class=\"m\">[0, 2\\pi]</span> gives <span class=\"m\">2\\pi r^2</span>. The flux form of Green's theorem relates outflow through the boundary to the integral of divergence, the 2D version of the divergence theorem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"common-mistakes\" data-h=\"3\">Common mistakes</h3>\n<p>Confusing divergence (a sum of \"own\" partials, <span class=\"m\">P_x + Q_y</span>) with curl (cross partials, <span class=\"m\">Q_x - P_y</span>) is common. So is thinking that parallel arrows mean zero curl; shear flows prove otherwise. Use the endpoint shortcut only after checking that the field is conservative, and orient Green's theorem counterclockwise.</p>\n<h3 id=\"summary\" data-h=\"3\">Summary</h3>\n<p>A vector field attaches an arrow to every point. Gradient (conservative) fields have potentials, zero curl, and path-independent line integrals given by <span class=\"m\">\\varphi(\\text{end}) - \\varphi(\\text{start})</span>. The line integral <span class=\"m\">\\int\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r</span> measures work or circulation along a path. Divergence <span class=\"m\">P_x + Q_y</span> measures spreading, curl <span class=\"m\">Q_x - P_y</span> measures swirling, and Green's theorem connects the circulation around a loop to the total curl inside.</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n</section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-visual\" id=\"visual\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">2</span><div><h2>Visualize</h2><p>Play with the idea. Each visual comes with a guide on what to try and what to notice.</p></div></div><figure class=\"viz\" data-viz=\"g04-vector-field\" data-args='{&quot;field&quot;: &quot;rotation&quot;}'><div class=\"viz-body\"></div><figcaption><b>Try this:</b> Drag the orange point. The panel shows the field, its divergence and curl, and the circulation and flux around the orange loop.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box explore\"><div class=\"box-label\">How to use this visual</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><strong>What to try</strong>\n1. With the rotation field, read the divergence and curl at several points, then change the loop radius <span class=\"m\">r</span> and watch the circulation.\n2. Press Flow to release particles and watch their paths.\n3. Switch to the source, the sink, the gradient field <span class=\"m\">(y, x)</span> and the shear field, reading divergence and curl for each.\n4. Try the spiral sink.</p>\n<p><strong>What to notice:</strong> the rotation field has divergence 0 and curl 2 everywhere, and its circulation around the loop is <span class=\"m\">2\\pi r^2</span>, exactly as Green's theorem predicted in the article. The source has divergence 2 and its particles stream outward. The gradient field <span class=\"m\">(y, x)</span> has curl 0 and zero circulation around every loop, as a conservative field must. The shear field's arrows are parallel yet its curl is <span class=\"m\">-1</span>. The spiral sink mixes both: negative divergence and positive curl.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-formulas\" id=\"formulas\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">3</span><div><h2>Formulas & theorems</h2><p>The key results to remember, with proofs.</p></div></div><div class=\"box formulas\"><div class=\"box-label\">Key formulas</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p><span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf F = (P, Q) \\qquad \\nabla\\varphi = (\\varphi_x, \\varphi_y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_C\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot d\\mathbf r = \\varphi(\\mathbf r(b)) - \\varphi(\\mathbf r(a))</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F = P_x + Q_y \\qquad \\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F = Q_x - P_y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\oint_C P\\,dx + Q\\,dy = \\iint_D(Q_x - P_y)\\,dA \\qquad \\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds = \\iint_D(P_x + Q_y)\\,dA</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{curl}(\\nabla\\varphi) = 0 \\qquad \\nabla\\times\\mathbf F = (R_y - Q_z,\\ P_z - R_x,\\ Q_x - P_y) \\text{ in 3D}</span></p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Fundamental theorem for line integrals</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span> has continuous first partials and <span class=\"m\">C</span> is a smooth curve from <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf A</span> to <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf B</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\int_C\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot d\\mathbf r = \\varphi(\\mathbf B) - \\varphi(\\mathbf A)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box proof\"><div class=\"box-label\">Proof</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Parametrize <span class=\"m\">C</span> by <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf r(t)</span>, <span class=\"m\">a \\le t \\le b</span>. By the multivariable chain rule, <span class=\"m\">\\frac{d}{dt}\\varphi(\\mathbf r(t)) = \\nabla\\varphi(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)</span>. So the line integral is <span class=\"m\">\\int_a^b\\frac{d}{dt}\\varphi(\\mathbf r(t))\\,dt</span>, which by the single-variable fundamental theorem of calculus equals <span class=\"m\">\\varphi(\\mathbf r(b)) - \\varphi(\\mathbf r(a))</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box thm\"><div class=\"box-label\">Theorem</div><div class=\"box-title\">Green's theorem</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>If <span class=\"m\">C</span> is a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth simple closed curve bounding a region <span class=\"m\">D</span>, and <span class=\"m\">P</span>, <span class=\"m\">Q</span> have continuous first partials on an open set containing <span class=\"m\">D</span>, then <span class=\"m\">\\oint_C P\\,dx + Q\\,dy = \\iint_D(Q_x - P_y)\\,dA</span>.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-examples\" id=\"examples\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">4</span><div><h2>Worked examples</h2><p>Problems solved step by step. Try each one before opening the steps.</p></div></div><div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Divergence and curl of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (x^2y,\\ xy)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">P = x^2y</span>, <span class=\"m\">Q = xy</span>: <span class=\"m\">P_x = 2xy</span>, <span class=\"m\">Q_y = x</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F = 2xy + x</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">Q_x = y</span>, <span class=\"m\">P_y = x^2</span>, so <span class=\"m\">\\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F = y - x^2</span>.</li>\n<li>At <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>: divergence <span class=\"m\">5</span>, curl <span class=\"m\">1</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Work done by <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (2x, 2y)</span> along any path from <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(3, 4)</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = \\nabla(x^2 + y^2)</span>, so the field is conservative.</li>\n<li>Work <span class=\"m\">= \\varphi(3, 4) - \\varphi(0, 0) = 25 - 0</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">W = 25</span>, whatever the path.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box steps\"><div class=\"box-label\">Step by step</div><div class=\"box-title\">Circulation of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (-y, x)</span> around the unit square <span class=\"m\">[0, 1]^2</span></div><div class=\"box-body\"><ol>\n<li>Curl <span class=\"m\">= Q_x - P_y = 1 - (-1) = 2</span>.</li>\n<li>Green: circulation <span class=\"m\">= \\iint_D2\\,dA = 2\\times\\text{area}</span>.</li>\n<li><span class=\"m\">= 2</span>.</li>\n</ol></div></div>\n<div class=\"box mock\"><div class=\"box-label\">Exam-style question</div><div class=\"box-title\">Exam question</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Let <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (2xy,\\ x^2 + 2y)</span>. (a) Show that <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F</span> is conservative. (b) Find a potential <span class=\"m\">\\varphi</span>. (c) Find the work done by <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F</span> moving a particle from <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(1, 2)</span>. (d) What is the work around any closed loop?</p>\n<details class=\"reveal solution\"><summary>Show solution</summary><div><p>(a) <span class=\"m\">P_y = 2x</span> and <span class=\"m\">Q_x = 2x</span> are equal (and the domain is all of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbb R^2</span>), so the curl is 0 and <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F</span> is conservative. (b) <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_x = 2xy</span> gives <span class=\"m\">\\varphi = x^2y + h(y)</span>; then <span class=\"m\">\\varphi_y = x^2 + h'(y) = x^2 + 2y</span>, so <span class=\"m\">h = y^2</span> and <span class=\"m\">\\varphi = x^2y + y^2</span>. (c) <span class=\"m\">W = \\varphi(1, 2) - \\varphi(0, 0) = 2 + 4 = 6</span>. (d) Zero.</p></div></details></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-apply\" id=\"apply\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">5</span><div><h2>Where it's used</h2><p>How this shows up in machine learning, computer science and real life.</p></div></div><div class=\"box ml\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for ML</div><div class=\"box-title\">Gradient fields and flows in ML</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Gradient descent follows the vector field <span class=\"m\">-\\nabla L</span> over weight space; its flow lines are the paths of gradient flow. Score-based diffusion models learn the vector field <span class=\"m\">\\nabla_{\\mathbf x}\\log p(\\mathbf x)</span>, the gradient of the log-density, and generate samples by following it with noise. Neural ODEs and flow-matching models learn a velocity field and transport samples along its flow lines; the divergence of that field tracks how probability density changes along the way.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box cs\"><div class=\"box-label\">Why it matters for CS</div><div class=\"box-title\">Simulating fluids and fields</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Physics engines and weather models store vector fields on grids and compute divergence and curl with finite differences. Fluid simulations enforce incompressibility by removing the divergence of the velocity field at each step, solving a Poisson equation. Game AI uses flow fields, precomputed vectors pointing toward a goal, to steer thousands of units at once.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"box real\"><div class=\"box-label\">Real-life example</div><div class=\"box-title\">Wind and ocean currents</div><div class=\"box-body\"><p>Meteorologists compute the curl (vorticity) of wind fields to spot rotating storms: large positive vorticity in the northern hemisphere signals a cyclone. Divergence of the wind at high altitude, which pulls air upward from below, is a key ingredient in forecasting thunderstorms.</p></div></div></section>\n<section class=\"lsec lsec-practice\" id=\"practice\"><div class=\"lsec-head\"><span class=\"lsec-num\">6</span><div><h2>Practice</h2><p>Check your understanding. Every answer comes with an explanation.</p></div></div><div class=\"quiz\">\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"1\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 1 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The divergence of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (x, y)</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">1 + 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"2\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 2 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The curl of <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (-y, x)</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"2\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">Q_x - P_y = 1 - (-1)</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"3\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 3 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The curl of the shear field <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (y, 0)</span> is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"-1\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">0 - 1</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"4\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 4 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">The work done by <span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = (y, x)</span> from <span class=\"m\">(0, 0)</span> to <span class=\"m\">(2, 3)</span> along any path is...</div>\n<div class=\"qinput\"><input type=\"text\" inputmode=\"decimal\" placeholder=\"Your answer\" data-ans=\"6\"><button class=\"btn small qcheck\">Check</button></div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\mathbf F = \\nabla(xy)</span>, so <span class=\"m\">W = 2\\cdot3</span>.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"5\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 5 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">A gradient field always has...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">zero curl</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">zero divergence</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">constant length</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p>Mixed partials are equal.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"qcard\" data-q=\"6\"><div class=\"qnum\">Question 6 of 6</div><div class=\"qtext\">Green's theorem relates a line integral around a closed curve to...</div>\n<div class=\"qopts\">\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"1\">a double integral of the curl over the enclosed region</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the length of the curve</button>\n<button class=\"qopt\" data-ok=\"0\">the value of the field at one point</button>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qfeedback\"></div><div class=\"qexp\"><p><span class=\"m\">\\oint P\\,dx + Q\\,dy = \\iint(Q_x - P_y)\\,dA</span>.</p></div></div>\n</div></section>"}, "formulas": [{"l": "multivar-functions", "t": "Surfaces, contours and level sets", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">f : \\mathbb R^n \\to \\mathbb R,\\quad \\mathbf x = (x_1, \\ldots, x_n) \\mapsto f(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{graph: } \\{(x, y, z) : z = f(x, y)\\} \\qquad \\text{level set: } \\{(x, y) : f(x, y) = c\\}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">x^2 + y^2 = c \\text{ (circles)} \\qquad x^2 - y^2 = c \\text{ (hyperbolas)} \\qquad ax + by = c \\text{ (parallel lines)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\lim_{(x,y)\\to(a,b)}f(x, y) = L \\text{ must hold along every path to } (a, b)</span></p>"}, {"l": "partials", "t": "Partial derivatives", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">f_x(a, b) = \\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a + h, b) - f(a, b)}{h} \\qquad f_y(a, b) = \\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{f(a, b + h) - f(a, b)}{h}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f_x \\approx \\frac{f(x + h, y) - f(x, y)}{h} \\qquad f_x \\approx \\frac{f(x + h, y) - f(x - h, y)}{2h}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f_{xx} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial x^2} \\qquad f_{xy} = \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\frac{\\partial^2f}{\\partial y\\,\\partial x}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f_{xy} = f_{yx} \\text{ (Clairaut, when continuous)}</span></p>"}, {"l": "tangent-planes", "t": "Tangent planes and linear approximation", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">z = f(a, b) + f_x(a, b)(x - a) + f_y(a, b)(y - b)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">L(\\mathbf x) = f(\\mathbf a) + \\nabla f(\\mathbf a)\\cdot(\\mathbf x - \\mathbf a)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">df = f_x\\,dx + f_y\\,dy \\qquad \\Delta f \\approx df</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(x, y, z) = c:\\ F_x(a, b, c_0)(x - a) + F_y(\\cdot)(y - b) + F_z(\\cdot)(z - c_0) = 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dA}{A} = \\frac{dx}{x} + \\frac{dy}{y} \\text{ for } A = xy</span></p>"}, {"l": "gradient", "t": "The gradient and directional derivatives", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = (f_x, f_y) \\qquad \\nabla f = \\left(\\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_1}, \\ldots, \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x_n}\\right)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D_{\\mathbf u}f = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf u = \\|\\nabla f\\|\\cos\\theta\\quad(\\|\\mathbf u\\| = 1)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\max_{\\mathbf u}D_{\\mathbf u}f = \\|\\nabla f\\| \\text{ in direction } \\frac{\\nabla f}{\\|\\nabla f\\|}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf x_{k+1} = \\mathbf x_k - \\eta\\,\\nabla f(\\mathbf x_k)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla(f + g) = \\nabla f + \\nabla g \\qquad \\nabla(fg) = g\\nabla f + f\\nabla g \\qquad \\nabla(\\mathbf w\\cdot\\mathbf x) = \\mathbf w \\qquad \\nabla\\|\\mathbf x\\|^2 = 2\\mathbf x</span></p>"}, {"l": "multi-chain", "t": "The multivariable chain rule", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dz}{dt} = \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\frac{dx}{dt} + \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\nabla f\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial s} = \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x}\\frac{\\partial x}{\\partial s} + \\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial y}\\frac{\\partial y}{\\partial s}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial t_j} = \\sum_i\\frac{\\partial z}{\\partial x_i}\\frac{\\partial x_i}{\\partial t_j}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">F(x, y) = 0 \\Rightarrow \\frac{dy}{dx} = -\\frac{F_x}{F_y}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial v} = \\sum_{c\\,\\in\\,\\text{children}(v)}\\frac{\\partial L}{\\partial c}\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial v}</span></p>"}, {"l": "jacobian-hessian", "t": "Jacobians and Hessians", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">J_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial F_i}{\\partial x_j} \\qquad \\mathbf F(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) \\approx \\mathbf F(\\mathbf a) + J(\\mathbf a)\\mathbf h</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">J_{\\mathbf F\\circ\\mathbf G} = J_{\\mathbf F}(\\mathbf G(\\mathbf x))\\,J_{\\mathbf G}(\\mathbf x)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H_{ij} = \\frac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x_i\\partial x_j},\\quad H = H^\\top,\\quad H = J_{\\nabla f}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">f(\\mathbf a + \\mathbf h) \\approx f(\\mathbf a) + \\nabla f^\\top\\mathbf h + \\tfrac12\\mathbf h^\\top H\\mathbf h</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla(\\tfrac12\\mathbf x^\\top A\\mathbf x) = A\\mathbf x,\\quad H = A \\quad (A \\text{ symmetric})</span></p>"}, {"l": "multi-optimization", "t": "Optimization in several variables", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f(\\mathbf a) = \\mathbf 0 \\text{ at interior extrema}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D = f_{xx}f_{yy} - f_{xy}^2 = \\det H</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">D &gt; 0, f_{xx} &gt; 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{min} \\qquad D &gt; 0, f_{xx} &lt; 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{max} \\qquad D &lt; 0 \\Rightarrow \\text{saddle}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{least squares: } X^\\top X\\,\\mathbf w = X^\\top\\mathbf y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">H \\text{ positive definite everywhere} \\Rightarrow f \\text{ convex} \\Rightarrow \\text{critical point is the global min}</span></p>"}, {"l": "lagrange", "t": "Lagrange multipliers", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = \\lambda\\nabla g, \\quad g(\\mathbf x) = c</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\mathcal L(\\mathbf x, \\lambda) = f(\\mathbf x) - \\lambda(g(\\mathbf x) - c), \\quad \\nabla_{\\mathbf x, \\lambda}\\mathcal L = \\mathbf 0</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\nabla f = \\lambda_1\\nabla g_1 + \\lambda_2\\nabla g_2 \\text{ (two constraints)}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\frac{dM}{dc} = \\lambda</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\max_{\\|\\mathbf x\\| = 1}\\mathbf a\\cdot\\mathbf x = \\|\\mathbf a\\| \\qquad \\max_{\\|\\mathbf x\\| = 1}\\mathbf x^\\top A\\mathbf x = \\lambda_{\\max}(A)</span></p>"}, {"l": "multiple-integrals", "t": "Double and triple integrals", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\iint_R f\\,dA = \\lim\\sum f(x_i, y_j)\\,\\Delta A</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_{[a,b]\\times[c,d]}f\\,dA = \\int_a^b\\int_c^d f\\,dy\\,dx = \\int_c^d\\int_a^b f\\,dx\\,dy</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_D f\\,dA = \\int_a^b\\int_{g_1(x)}^{g_2(x)}f\\,dy\\,dx</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{area}(D) = \\iint_D1\\,dA \\qquad \\bar f = \\frac{1}{\\text{area}(D)}\\iint_D f\\,dA</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\iint_{[a,b]\\times[c,d]}g(x)h(y)\\,dA = \\left(\\int_a^b g\\right)\\left(\\int_c^d h\\right)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">P((X, Y)\\in D) = \\iint_D p\\,dA \\qquad \\mathbb E[g(X, Y)] = \\iint g\\,p\\,dA</span></p>"}, {"l": "change-of-variables", "t": "Change of variables: polar, cylindrical and spherical coordinates", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\iint_D f\\,dx\\,dy = \\iint_{D'}f(x(u, v), y(u, v))\\left|\\det\\frac{\\partial(x, y)}{\\partial(u, v)}\\right|du\\,dv</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{polar: } dA = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta \\qquad \\text{cylindrical: } dV = r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\,dz</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\text{spherical: } dV = \\rho^2\\sin\\varphi\\,d\\rho\\,d\\varphi\\,d\\theta</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2}dx = \\sqrt\\pi \\qquad \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}e^{-x^2/2}dx = \\sqrt{2\\pi}</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">p_Y(\\mathbf y) = p_X(T^{-1}(\\mathbf y))\\left|\\det J_{T^{-1}}(\\mathbf y)\\right|</span></p>"}, {"l": "vector-fields", "t": "Vector fields, divergence and curl", "html": "<p><span class=\"mb\">\\mathbf F = (P, Q) \\qquad \\nabla\\varphi = (\\varphi_x, \\varphi_y)</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot d\\mathbf r = \\int_a^b\\mathbf F(\\mathbf r(t))\\cdot\\mathbf r'(t)\\,dt</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\int_C\\nabla\\varphi\\cdot d\\mathbf r = \\varphi(\\mathbf r(b)) - \\varphi(\\mathbf r(a))</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{div}\\mathbf F = P_x + Q_y \\qquad \\operatorname{curl}\\mathbf F = Q_x - P_y</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\oint_C P\\,dx + Q\\,dy = \\iint_D(Q_x - P_y)\\,dA \\qquad \\oint_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\mathbf n\\,ds = \\iint_D(P_x + Q_y)\\,dA</span>\n<span class=\"mb\">\\operatorname{curl}(\\nabla\\varphi) = 0 \\qquad \\nabla\\times\\mathbf F = (R_y - Q_z,\\ P_z - R_x,\\ Q_x - P_y) \\text{ in 3D}</span></p>"}]});
